See Also  \aCHR$\vCHR$\v  \aASC\vASC\v  \aKeyboard Scan Codes\vscancodes\v
.context scancodes
.topic Keyboard Scan Codes
The codes ON KEY and INP(&H60) see for each key (decimal):

    Esc  1        F1  59     Q  16    A  30    Z  44    Home   71
    1    2        F2  60     W  17    S  31    X  45    Up     72
    2    3        F3  61     E  18    D  32    C  46    PgUp   73
    3    4        F4  62     R  19    F  33    V  47    Grey - 74
    4    5        F5  63     T  20    G  34    B  48    Left   75
    5    6        F6  64     Y  21    H  35    N  49    Pad 5  76
    6    7        F7  65     U  22    J  36    M  50    Right  77
    7    8        F8  66     I  23    K  37    ,  51    Grey + 78
    8    9        F9  67     O  24    L  38    .  52    End    79
    9    10       F10 68     P  25    ;  39    /  53    Down   80
    0    11       F11 87     [  26    '  40    Space 57 PgDn   81
    -    12       F12 88     ]  27    `  41    Caps  58 Ins    82
    =    13       Enter 28   \\  43    Ctrl  29 Alt   56 Del    83
    Backspace 14  Tab 15     L.Shift 42  R.Shift 54  Num Lock 69
    Scroll Lock 70

INKEY$ gives an extended key - an arrow, a function key - as two
characters, CHR$(0) and then the scan code.

See Also  \aINKEY$\vINKEY$\v  \aON KEY\vON-KEY\v  \aINP\vINP\v
.context m.menus
.topic Menus
Alt lights the menu bar.  A highlighted letter, or Left
and Right and then Enter, opens a menu; Alt with the letter
opens it at once.  In a menu, Up and Down move to
an item, Enter or the item's letter chooses the item, and
Esc closes the menu.  The mouse can click a menu and drag
to an item.

A grey item cannot be chosen yet: Cut, for one, needs a
selection.  "..." after an item means a dialog follows.
.context m.message
.topic Messages
The message says why QBasic stopped, or asks a question.
Enter chooses the lit button, Tab moves to the next one,
and Esc closes the box without choosing.
.context m.new
.topic New Command
Clears the program away, the module and all its SUBs and
FUNCTIONs, so that a new one can be typed.  If the old one
has changes that are not saved, QBasic asks first.
.context m.open
.topic Open Command
Loads a program from a disk in place of the one in memory.
The dialog lists the .BAS files of a directory; a name, a
path or a wildcard can be typed in the File Name box.
.context m.save
.topic Save Command
Writes the program to the file it was loaded from.  A
program not yet saved is given a name first, as Save As
does.
.context m.saveas
.topic Save As Command
Writes the program to a file under a name typed in the
dialog, and from then on the program has that name.
.context m.print
.topic Print Command
Prints the selected text, the window's text (the module or
one procedure) or the whole program on LPT1.
.context m.exit
.topic Exit Command
Leaves QBasic for DOS.  If the program has changes that are
not saved, QBasic asks first.
.context m.cut
.topic Cut Command
Takes the selected text out of the program and keeps it on
the clipboard, from where Paste can put it back.
Shortcut: Shift+Del.
.context m.copy
.topic Copy Command
Puts a copy of the selected text on the clipboard.  The
program is not changed.  Shortcut: Ctrl+Ins.
.context m.paste
.topic Paste Command
Puts the clipboard's text in at the cursor, or in place of
the selected text.  Shortcut: Shift+Ins.
.context m.clear
.topic Clear Command
Deletes the selected text.  The clipboard is not changed.
Shortcut: Del.
.context m.newsub
.topic New SUB Command
Asks for a name and starts a SUB procedure of that name in
a window of its own.  Typing SUB and a name on a line of
the program does the same.
.context m.newfunc
.topic New FUNCTION Command
Asks for a name and starts a FUNCTION procedure of that
name in a window of its own.  Typing FUNCTION and a name on
a line of the program does the same.
.context m.subs
.topic SUBs Command
Lists the module and its SUBs and FUNCTIONs.  The one chosen
is shown in the window; a procedure can be deleted from
here.  Shortcut: F2.
.context m.split
.topic Split Command
Divides the editing window in two, so that two parts of the
program - or two procedures - can be seen at once.  Split
again goes back to one window.  F6 moves between windows.
.context m.output
.topic Output Screen Command
Shows the screen the program writes on.  A key comes back
to QBasic.  Shortcut: F4.
.context m.find
.topic Find Command
Looks for some text in the program, from the cursor on.  The
dialog says whether case must match and whether the text
must be a whole word.
.context m.repeat
.topic Repeat Last Find Command
Looks for the text of the last Find again, from the cursor
on.  Shortcut: F3.
.context m.change
.topic Change Command
Looks for some text and puts other text in its place, one
place at a time with a question each time, or everywhere at
once.
.context m.start
.topic Start Command
Runs the program from its first statement, with every
variable cleared.  Shortcut: Shift+F5.
.context m.restart
.topic Restart Command
Clears every variable and makes the first statement the
next to run, without running it.  F8 then steps from the
start.
.context m.continue
.topic Continue Command
Runs the program on from where it stopped: a STOP, a
breakpoint, Ctrl+Break, an error, or a step.  A program not
yet started starts from the beginning.  Shortcut: F5.
.context m.step
.topic Step Command
Runs the next statement and stops.  A statement that calls
a SUB or FUNCTION steps into the procedure.  Shortcut: F8.
.context m.pstep
.topic Procedure Step Command
Runs the next statement and stops, but runs a procedure
call to its end in one step.  Shortcut: F10.
.context m.trace
.topic Trace On Command
While Trace On is checked, a running program lights each
statement in the window as it runs it, slowly.  Choose it
again to turn the tracing off.
.context m.breakpoint
.topic Toggle Breakpoint Command
Puts a breakpoint on the cursor's line, or takes one away.
A running program stops before the line's first statement.
Shortcut: F9.
.context m.clearbp
.topic Clear All Breakpoints Command
Takes away every breakpoint in the program.
.context m.setnext
.topic Set Next Statement Command
Makes the statement on the cursor's line the next one to
run.  The program must be stopped, and the line must be in
the procedure it is stopped in.
.context m.display
.topic Display Command
Sets the colours of the program text, of the statement the
program is stopped at, and of breakpoint lines; whether the
windows have scroll bars; and how far apart tab stops are.
.context m.helppath
.topic Help Path Command
Says which directory QBASIC.HLP is in, when it is not
beside QBASIC.EXE, in PM-DOS's own directory, in the
current directory or on the PATH.
.context m.syntax
.topic Syntax Checking Command
While Syntax Checking is on, each line is checked as the
cursor leaves it: keywords become capitals and a mistake is
shown at once.  Turned off, lines are left as typed until
the program runs.
.context m.index
.topic Index Command
Shows the help Index: every keyword, A to Z.  A letter key
moves to that letter; Enter or F1 opens the keyword's help.
.context m.contents
.topic Contents Command
Shows the help Contents: the help topics in groups.
.context m.topic
.topic Topic Command
Shows the help on the keyword the cursor is on.  For a name
of the program's own, help says what the name is.
Shortcut: F1.
.context m.usinghelp
.topic Using Help Command
Shows how to use the help.  Shortcut: Shift+F1.
.context m.about
.topic About Command
Shows the version of QBasic.
.context d.open
.topic Open Dialog
\bFile Name\p  The program to load.  A wildcard such as *.BAS
           fills the Files list instead; a directory or
           a drive moves there.
\bFiles\p      The files that match.  Enter or a double click
           loads one.
\bDirs/Drives\p  Another directory or drive to look in.

Tab moves between the parts of the dialog; Esc cancels.
.context d.saveas
.topic Save As Dialog
\bFile Name\p  The name to save the program under.  .BAS is
           added when no extension is given.  A path
           puts the file in another directory.

The directory shown is where the file goes without a path.
.context d.print
.topic Print Dialog
Choose what to print on LPT1:

\bSelected Text Only\p  The text selected in the window.
\bCurrent Window\p      All of the window's text: the module or
                    the procedure shown.
\bEntire Program\p      The module and every procedure.
.context d.newsub
.topic New SUB Dialog
\bName\p  The SUB's name: a letter, then letters, digits and
      dots, up to 40 characters.  The new SUB gets a
      window of its own.
.context d.newfunc
.topic New FUNCTION Dialog
\bName\p  The FUNCTION's name: a letter, then letters, digits
      and dots, up to 40 characters, with a type suffix if
      the function returns a string or another type.  The
      new FUNCTION gets a window of its own.
.context d.subs
.topic SUBs Dialog
The list holds the module first, then its procedures.

\bEdit in Active\p  Shows the chosen one in the window.
\bDelete\p          Deletes the chosen procedure from the
                program, after a question.
\bCancel\p          Closes the dialog.
.context d.find
.topic Find Dialog
\bFind What\p         The text to look for.  It starts as the
                  word at the cursor or the selection.
\bMatch Upper/Lowercase\p  Case must be as typed.
\bWhole Word\p        The text must not be part of a longer
                  word.
.context d.change
.topic Change Dialog
\bFind What\p    The text to look for.
\bChange To\p    What goes in its place.
\bFind and Verify\p  Shows each place in turn and asks
                 whether to change it.
\bChange All\p   Changes every place without asking.
.context d.verify
.topic Change, Skip Dialog
The found text is selected in the window.

\bChange\p  Puts the new text in its place and finds the next.
\bSkip\p    Leaves it and finds the next.
\bCancel\p  Stops changing.
.context d.display
.topic Display Dialog
\bColors\p  Choose what to colour - Normal Text, Current
        Statement or Breakpoint Lines - then its foreground
        and background colours from the lists.
\bScroll Bars\p  Whether the windows show scroll bars.
\bTab Stops\p    How many columns apart tab stops are.
.context d.helppath
.topic Help Path Dialog
The directory that holds QBASIC.HLP, for example
C:\\DOS.  Left empty, QBasic looks beside QBASIC.EXE, in
PM-DOS's own directory, in the current directory, and
along the PATH.
.context d.about
.topic About QBasic
The version of QBasic for PM-DOS.
.context errcodes
.topic Run-Time Error Codes
The numbers ERR returns, and the messages that go with them.  ON ERROR
GOTO catches every one of them; ERROR n makes one happen.

     1  NEXT without FOR               37  Argument-count mismatch
     2  Syntax error                   38  Array not defined
     3  RETURN without GOSUB           40  Variable required
     4  Out of DATA                    50  FIELD overflow
     5  Illegal function call          51  Internal error
     6  Overflow                       52  Bad file name or number
     7  Out of memory                  53  File not found
     8  Label not defined              54  Bad file mode
     9  Subscript out of range         55  File already open
    10  Duplicate definition           56  FIELD statement active
    11  Division by zero               57  Device I/O error
    12  Illegal in direct mode         58  File already exists
    13  Type mismatch                  59  Bad record length
    14  Out of string space            61  Disk full
    16  String formula too complex     62  Input past end of file
    17  Cannot continue                63  Bad record number
    18  Function not defined           64  Bad file name
    19  No RESUME                      67  Too many files
    20  RESUME without error           68  Device unavailable
    24  Device timeout                 69  Communication-buffer overflow
    25  Device fault                   70  Permission denied
    26  FOR without NEXT               71  Disk not ready
    27  Out of paper                   72  Disk-media error
    28  Out of stack space             73  Advanced feature unavailable
    29  WHILE without WEND             74  Rename across disks
    30  WEND without WHILE             75  Path/File access error
    33  Duplicate label                76  Path not found
    35  Subprogram not defined

Any other number given to ERROR shows "Unprintable error".

See Also  \aERR\vERR\v  \aERROR\vERROR\v  \aON ERROR\vON-ERROR\v  \aRESUME\vRESUME\v
.context NEXT without FOR
.topic NEXT without FOR
A NEXT has no FOR to go back to, or names a different
variable from the FOR it closes.

ERR code: 1
.context Syntax error
.topic Syntax error
The line is not a statement QBasic can read: a word is
misspelt, a part is missing, or something is in the wrong
order.  The cursor is put where the reading stopped.  READ
gives this error, too, when a DATA item is not a number and
a number is wanted.

ERR code: 2
.context RETURN without GOSUB
.topic RETURN without GOSUB
A RETURN ran with no GOSUB waiting for it - often because
the program ran into a subroutine instead of reaching it by
GOSUB.  An END or a GOTO before the subroutine's label stops
that.

ERR code: 3
.context Out of DATA
.topic Out of DATA
READ was asked for an item after the last DATA item.
RESTORE starts the items again.

ERR code: 4
.context Illegal function call
.topic Illegal function call
A value given to a statement or function is outside what it
takes: a negative square root, MID$ starting at 0, a colour
that does not exist, a screen position off the screen.

ERR code: 5
.context Overflow
.topic Overflow
A result is too big for its type: more than 32,767 for an
INTEGER, more than 2,147,483,647 for a LONG, or beyond the
range of a SINGLE or DOUBLE.  A suffix on a variable, or
CLNG or CDBL, gives the result a bigger type.

ERR code: 6
.context Out of memory
.topic Out of memory
There is no memory left for the program, its arrays or its
strings.  Smaller arrays, ERASE for arrays no longer needed,
or fewer programs loaded in memory leave more.

ERR code: 7
.context Label not defined
.topic Label not defined
A GOTO, GOSUB, RESTORE, RESUME or ON ...  GOTO names a label
or line number that is not in the program - or not in the
same procedure, as labels in a SUB or FUNCTION are its own.

ERR code: 8
.context Subscript out of range
.topic Subscript out of range
An array element was used with a subscript outside the
array's bounds, or an array was used with more subscripts
than it has dimensions.  An array used without DIM has
subscripts 0 to 10.

ERR code: 9
.context Duplicate definition
.topic Duplicate definition
A name is declared twice: an array dimensioned again without
ERASE, a CONST or variable with the name of another, a
second OPTION BASE, a TYPE element named twice.

ERR code: 10
.context Division by zero
.topic Division by zero
A number was divided by zero (/, \\ or MOD), or zero was
raised to a negative power.

ERR code: 11
.context Illegal in direct mode
.topic Illegal in direct mode
The statement cannot be typed in the Immediate window: it
declares something (DIM, CONST, DECLARE, SUB, TYPE, DATA...)
or has a label.  Put it in the program instead.

ERR code: 12
.context Type mismatch
.topic Type mismatch
A string was used where a number belongs, or a number where
a string belongs: in an assignment, as an argument, or in a
comparison.

ERR code: 13
.context Out of string space
.topic Out of string space
The strings of the program fill the memory there is for
them.

ERR code: 14
.context String formula too complex
.topic String formula too complex
A string expression is too long or deep to be worked out.
Splitting it into steps with variables in between helps.

ERR code: 16
.context Cannot continue
.topic Cannot continue
The program was changed in a way that stops it from going on
where it stopped.  Start it again with Shift+F5.

ERR code: 17
.context Function not defined
.topic Function not defined
An FN function was used before its DEF FN, or a FUNCTION was
called that the program does not have.

ERR code: 18
.context No RESUME
.topic No RESUME
An error handler reached the end of the program without a
RESUME.

ERR code: 19
.context RESUME without error
.topic RESUME without error
RESUME ran when no error was being handled - for instance
when the program ran into its error handler instead of being
sent there by an error.

ERR code: 20
.context Device timeout
.topic Device timeout
A device - the printer or a serial port - did not answer in
time.

ERR code: 24
.context Device fault
.topic Device fault
A device reported a fault, or a serial port lost its
signals.

ERR code: 25
.context FOR without NEXT
.topic FOR without NEXT
A FOR has no NEXT to close its loop.

ERR code: 26
.context Out of paper
.topic Out of paper
The printer is out of paper or not switched on.

ERR code: 27
.context Out of stack space
.topic Out of stack space
Procedures call each other, or themselves, too deeply, or
GOSUBs nest without their RETURNs.

ERR code: 28
.context WHILE without WEND
.topic WHILE without WEND
A WHILE has no WEND to close its loop.

ERR code: 29
.context WEND without WHILE
.topic WEND without WHILE
A WEND has no WHILE to go back to.

ERR code: 30
.context Duplicate label
.topic Duplicate label
Two lines in the same module or procedure have the same
label or line number.

ERR code: 33
.context Subprogram not defined
.topic Subprogram not defined
A CALL names a SUB the program does not have, or a statement
QBasic does not know is taken for a SUB call.

ERR code: 35
.context Argument-count mismatch
.topic Argument-count mismatch
A SUB or FUNCTION was called with more or fewer arguments
than it has parameters.

ERR code: 37
.context Array not defined
.topic Array not defined
An array was used that has not been dimensioned, or was
dimensioned with a different number of subscripts.

ERR code: 38
.context Variable required
.topic Variable required
A statement wants a variable to put its result in - INPUT,
READ, GET, LSET, SWAP - and was given an expression or a
constant.

ERR code: 40
.context FIELD overflow
.topic FIELD overflow
A FIELD statement asks for more bytes than the file's record
length, or GET or PUT went past them.

ERR code: 50
.context Internal error
.topic Internal error
Something went wrong inside QBasic itself.  The program
cannot go on.

ERR code: 51
.context Bad file name or number
.topic Bad file name or number
A file number is not one an OPEN gave, or is outside 1 to
255.

ERR code: 52
.context File not found
.topic File not found
The file named in OPEN FOR INPUT, KILL, NAME, CHAIN, RUN or
FILES is not there.

ERR code: 53
.context Bad file mode
.topic Bad file mode
The statement does not suit the way the file was opened:
PRINT # on a file opened for INPUT, GET on a sequential
file, INPUT # on one opened for OUTPUT.

ERR code: 54
.context File already open
.topic File already open
The file is open already: OPEN gave a file number in use, or
a file that is open was named in KILL or NAME.

ERR code: 55
.context FIELD statement active
.topic FIELD statement active
GET or PUT with a record variable was used on a file that
has a FIELD.

ERR code: 56
.context Device I/O error
.topic Device I/O error
A device or disk reported an error the program cannot get
past.

ERR code: 57
.context File already exists
.topic File already exists
NAME was asked to give a file a name another file has.

ERR code: 58
.context Bad record length
.topic Bad record length
A GET or PUT record variable is not the length the file was
opened with.

ERR code: 59
.context Disk full
.topic Disk full
There is no room left on the disk.

ERR code: 61
.context Input past end of file
.topic Input past end of file
INPUT # or LINE INPUT # read past the end of the file.  EOF
says when the end is reached.

ERR code: 62
.context Bad record number
.topic Bad record number
The record number of a GET or PUT is 0 or less.

ERR code: 63
.context Bad file name
.topic Bad file name
The file name has characters a file name cannot have, or is
empty.

ERR code: 64
.context Too many files
.topic Too many files
No more files can be open at once, or the directory is full.

ERR code: 67
.context Device unavailable
.topic Device unavailable
The device named is not there or not on line.

ERR code: 68
.context Communication-buffer overflow
.topic Communication-buffer overflow
A serial port received more than its buffer holds before the
program read it.

ERR code: 69
.context Permission denied
.topic Permission denied
The disk is write-protected, the file is read-only, or a
file shared by another program is locked.

ERR code: 70
.context Disk not ready
.topic Disk not ready
The drive has no disk in it, or its door is open.

ERR code: 71
.context Disk-media error
.topic Disk-media error
The disk has a fault where QBasic tried to read or write.

ERR code: 72
.context Advanced feature unavailable
.topic Advanced feature unavailable
The statement asks for something this QBasic does not do.
On PM-DOS that includes CALL ABSOLUTE, which would run
real-mode machine code.

ERR code: 73
.context Rename across disks
.topic Rename across disks
NAME can move a file only within one drive.

ERR code: 74
.context Path/File access error
.topic Path/File access error
The path and file could not be reached together: a file
opened as a directory, or a directory used as a file.

ERR code: 75
.context Path not found
.topic Path not found
A directory in the path given does not exist.

ERR code: 76
.context Unprintable error
.topic Unprintable error
An error happened that has no message: ERROR was given a
number QBasic does not use.
.context Array already dimensioned
.topic Array already dimensioned
The array has a DIM already, or was used before its DIM.
ERASE frees a dynamic array so it can be dimensioned again;
REDIM does both.
.context CASE without SELECT
.topic CASE without SELECT
A CASE is not inside a SELECT CASE block.
.context CASE ELSE must be the last CASE
.topic CASE ELSE must be the last CASE
A CASE came after the CASE ELSE of a SELECT CASE block.
.context Block IF without END IF
.topic Block IF without END IF
An IF ...  THEN that ends its line opens a block, and the
block has no END IF.
.context DO without LOOP
.topic DO without LOOP
A DO has no LOOP to close it.
.context SELECT without END SELECT
.topic SELECT without END SELECT
A SELECT CASE has no END SELECT.
.context SUB without END SUB
.topic SUB without END SUB
The SUB has no END SUB.
.context FUNCTION without END FUNCTION
.topic FUNCTION without END FUNCTION
The FUNCTION has no END FUNCTION.
.context DEF without END DEF
.topic DEF without END DEF
A DEF FN on more than one line has no END DEF.
.context ELSE without IF
.topic ELSE without IF
An ELSE is not inside an IF block, or follows the block's
ELSE.
.context ELSEIF without IF
.topic ELSEIF without IF
An ELSEIF is not inside an IF block, or follows the block's
ELSE.
.context END IF without block IF
.topic END IF without block IF
An END IF has no block IF to close.
.context END SELECT without SELECT
.topic END SELECT without SELECT
An END SELECT has no SELECT CASE to close.
.context END SUB without SUB
.topic END SUB without SUB
An END SUB is outside a SUB.
.context END FUNCTION without FUNCTION
.topic END FUNCTION without FUNCTION
An END FUNCTION is outside a FUNCTION.
.context END DEF without DEF
.topic END DEF without DEF
An END DEF has no DEF FN to close.
.context END TYPE without TYPE
.topic END TYPE without TYPE
An END TYPE has no TYPE to close.
.context TYPE without END TYPE
.topic TYPE without END TYPE
A TYPE has no END TYPE.
.context LOOP without DO
.topic LOOP without DO
A LOOP has no DO to go back to.
.context EXIT DO not within DO...LOOP
.topic EXIT DO not within DO...LOOP
EXIT DO is not inside a DO loop.
.context EXIT FOR not within FOR...NEXT
.topic EXIT FOR not within FOR...NEXT
EXIT FOR is not inside a FOR loop.
.context EXIT DEF not within DEF FN
.topic EXIT DEF not within DEF FN
EXIT DEF is not inside a DEF FN.
.context EXIT SUB not within SUB
.topic EXIT SUB not within SUB
EXIT SUB is in a FUNCTION or a DEF FN, not a SUB.
.context EXIT FUNCTION not within FUNCTION
.topic EXIT FUNCTION not within FUNCTION
EXIT FUNCTION is in a SUB or a DEF FN, not a FUNCTION.
.context Element not defined
.topic Element not defined
The record variable's TYPE has no element of that name.
.context Fixed-length string expected
.topic Fixed-length string expected
Only a fixed-length string (STRING * n) can be used here.
.context Fixed-length string not allowed here
.topic Fixed-length string not allowed here
A fixed-length string cannot be used here - for instance as
a parameter of a SUB or FUNCTION.
.context Illegal in procedure
.topic Illegal in procedure
The statement belongs in the module, outside every SUB and
FUNCTION: TYPE, DECLARE, COMMON, DEF FN and OPTION BASE
among others.
.context Illegal outside of SUB, FUNCTION, or DEF FN
.topic Illegal outside of SUB, FUNCTION, or DEF FN
STATIC as a statement, or EXIT DEF, belongs inside a
procedure.
.context Illegal outside of SUB/FUNCTION
.topic Illegal outside of SUB/FUNCTION
The statement belongs inside a SUB or FUNCTION: EXIT SUB,
EXIT FUNCTION, SHARED.
.context Invalid constant
.topic Invalid constant
A CONST, an array bound or a fixed string length was given
something that is not a constant, or a constant that does
not fit its type.
.context Invalid line number
.topic Invalid line number
Line numbers are whole numbers from 0 to 65,529.
.context Parameter type mismatch
.topic Parameter type mismatch
A variable passed to a SUB or FUNCTION is not the type of
the parameter it is passed to.  Put the variable in
parentheses to pass a copy of its value instead, or make the
types agree.
.context Program too complex
.topic Program too complex
The program, a statement in the program, or an expression is
too big for QBasic to hold.
.context SUB or FUNCTION expected
.topic SUB or FUNCTION expected
DECLARE and EXIT need SUB or FUNCTION after them.
.context SUB or FUNCTION not allowed here
.topic SUB or FUNCTION not allowed here
A SUB or FUNCTION starts inside another one, or inside a
block.
.context Statement cannot precede SUB/FUNCTION definition
.topic Statement cannot precede SUB/FUNCTION definition
Only remarks, blank lines and DEFtype statements may come
before a SUB or FUNCTION line in its window.
.context Statements cannot follow END SUB/FUNCTION
.topic Statements cannot follow END SUB/FUNCTION
Only remarks and blank lines may come after END SUB or END
FUNCTION.
.context Too many arguments
.topic Too many arguments
A function was given more arguments than it takes.
.context Too many dimensions
.topic Too many dimensions
An array may have at most 60 dimensions.
.context Too many elements
.topic Too many elements
A TYPE, or a list in a statement, has more parts than QBasic
allows.
.context Type not defined
.topic Type not defined
AS names a type that has no TYPE statement.
.context Wrong number of dimensions
.topic Wrong number of dimensions
The array was used with a different number of subscripts
from its DIM.
.context m.expected
.topic Expected
The line ended, or something else came, where QBasic needed
what the message names after "Expected:".  The cursor is put
where it was needed.
.context ABS
.topic ABS Function
ABS gives a number without its sign: its distance from zero.

\bABS\p(numeric-expression)

    þ numeric-expression   Any number.

The result has the type of the argument.  The most negative INTEGER
(-32768) and the most negative LONG (-2147483648) have no positive value
in their own type, so ABS of either is "Overflow".

Example:
    PRINT ABS(-7.5); ABS(12)          ' 7.5  12
    IF ABS(a - b) < .001 THEN PRINT "Close enough"

See Also  \aSGN\vSGN\v  \aINT\vINT\v  \aFIX\vFIX\v  \aArithmetic Operators\varith\v
.context ABSOLUTE
.topic CALL ABSOLUTE Statement
In QBasic, CALL ABSOLUTE runs machine code that the program has put in
memory, usually in an INTEGER array.

\bCALL ABSOLUTE\p ([argumentlist,] offset%)

    þ argumentlist   Values for the machine code.  Each goes on the
                     stack as the offset of the value.
    þ offset%        Where the code starts, in the segment DEF SEG set.

The machine code must end with a far return that also removes its
arguments from the stack.

In this QBasic there is no real mode to run 16-bit machine code in, so
CALL ABSOLUTE stops with "Advanced feature unavailable".  A program that
uses CALL ABSOLUTE still loads and can be edited; the error comes only
when the statement runs.

Example:
    DIM code%(20)
    ' ... machine code put into code%() with READ or POKE ...
    DEF SEG = VARSEG(code%(0))
    CALL ABSOLUTE(result%, VARPTR(code%(0)))
    DEF SEG

See Also  \aCALL\vCALL\v  \aDEF SEG\vSEG\v  \aVARPTR\vVARPTR\v  \aVARSEG\vVARSEG\v  \aQBasic on PM-DOS\vpmdos\v
.context ASC
.topic ASC Function
ASC gives the code (0 to 255) of the first character of a string.

\bASC\p(stringexpression$)

    þ stringexpression$   A string of at least one character.

ASC of an empty string is "Illegal function call".  CHR$ works the other
way round: it makes a character from a code.

Example:
    PRINT ASC("A"); ASC("a")          ' 65  97
    k$ = INKEY$
    IF LEN(k$) = 2 THEN PRINT "Scan code"; ASC(RIGHT$(k$, 1))

See Also  \aCHR$\vCHR$\v  \aINKEY$\vINKEY$\v  \aASCII Character Codes\vascii\v
.context ATN
.topic ATN Function
ATN gives the angle whose tangent is the number given.  The angle is in
radians, from -pi/2 to pi/2.

\bATN\p(numeric-expression)

    þ numeric-expression   The tangent: any number.

The result is a DOUBLE when the argument is a DOUBLE, otherwise a SINGLE.
Multiply radians by 180 / pi to get degrees.  4 * ATN(1) is pi.

Example:
    pi# = 4 * ATN(1#)
    PRINT ATN(1) * 180 / pi#          ' 45

See Also  \aTAN\vTAN\v  \aSIN\vSIN\v  \aCOS\vCOS\v
.context BEEP
.topic BEEP Statement
BEEP sounds the speaker at 800 Hz for a quarter of a second.

\bBEEP\p

The program waits until the beep, and any music still playing, is over.
PRINT CHR$(7) beeps the same way.

Example:
    IF score > best THEN BEEP: PRINT "New record"

See Also  \aSOUND\vSOUND\v  \aPLAY\vPLAY\v
.context BLOAD
.context BSAVE
.topic BLOAD, BSAVE Statements
BSAVE writes a block of memory to a file.  BLOAD reads such a file back
into memory.

\bBSAVE\p filespec$, offset%, length&
\bBLOAD\p filespec$ [, offset%]

    þ filespec$   The file.
    þ offset%     Where the block starts, in the segment DEF SEG set:
                  0 to 65535.
    þ length&     The number of bytes to save, 0 to 65535.

BSAVE puts 7 bytes in front of the block: &HFD, then the segment, the
offset and the length, a word each.  BLOAD without offset% puts the bytes
back at the segment and offset they were saved from; with offset%, at
that offset in the DEF SEG segment.

In this QBasic, BLOAD and BSAVE reach only what DEF SEG reaches: the
program's own variables, the screen at &HB800 and &HA000, and (to read)
the BIOS data area and the ROMs.

Example:
    DEF SEG = &HB800                  ' the text screen
    BSAVE "SCREEN.DAT", 0, 4000       ' 2,000 characters and colours
    BLOAD "SCREEN.DAT"                ' back where it was saved from
    DIM a(99)                         ' 100 SINGLEs, 400 bytes
    DEF SEG = VARSEG(a(0)): BSAVE "A.DAT", VARPTR(a(0)), 400
    DEF SEG

See Also  \aDEF SEG\vSEG\v  \aVARSEG\vVARSEG\v  \aVARPTR\vVARPTR\v  \aPEEK\vPEEK\v  \aPOKE\vPOKE\v
.context CALL
.topic CALL Statement
CALL runs a SUB procedure.  The word CALL may be left out.

\bCALL\p name [(argumentlist)]
name [argumentlist]

    þ name           The SUB's name.
    þ argumentlist   The values for the SUB's parameters, with commas
                     between them.  A whole array is written with empty
                     parentheses: list(), not list.

With CALL the arguments go in parentheses; without CALL they do not.

A variable, an array element or an array is passed by reference: what
the SUB does to its parameter is done to the variable.  Any other
expression is passed as a copy.  A variable in parentheses, (x), is an
expression, so the SUB gets a copy of x.

The wrong number of arguments is "Argument-count mismatch".  A variable
of the wrong type is "Parameter type mismatch"; an expression is
converted to the parameter's type.

Example:
    CALL Greet("Ann", 3)
    Greet "Ann", 3                    ' the same
    CALL Bump(n)                      ' Bump can change n
    CALL Bump((n))                    ' n stays as it is

See Also  \aSUB\vSUB\v  \aDECLARE\vDECLARE\v  \aFUNCTION\vFUNCTION\v  \aSHARED\vSHARED\v  \aCALL ABSOLUTE\vABSOLUTE\v
.context CDBL
.topic CDBL Function
CDBL gives a number as a DOUBLE (double precision).

\bCDBL\p(numeric-expression)

    þ numeric-expression   Any number.

CDBL changes the type, not the value.  A SINGLE such as .1 is not exact
in binary, and CDBL shows the SINGLE's small error: CDBL(.1) is
.1000000014901161.  Use CDBL to have an expression worked out in double
precision: CDBL(a%) / 3.

Example:
    PRINT 2 / 3, CDBL(2) / 3          ' .6666667  .6666666666666666

See Also  \aCSNG\vCSNG\v  \aCINT\vCINT\v  \aCLNG\vCLNG\v  \aData Types\vtypes\v
.context CHAIN
.topic CHAIN Statement
CHAIN stops the program that is running and starts another one, read
from a file.

\bCHAIN\p filespec$

    þ filespec$   The other program's file.  A name without an
                  extension gets .BAS.

The files stay open, and the variables named in COMMON statements in
both programs keep their values, matched by position.  All other
variables start again at 0 or "".  A named COMMON block (/name/) is
not passed on.

A file that is not there is "File not found", an error of the CHAIN
statement.  In the editor, the new program takes the old one's place.

Example:
    PRINT "Loading part 2..."
    CHAIN "PART2"                     ' PART2.BAS

See Also  \aCOMMON\vCOMMON\v  \aRUN\vRUN\v  \aSHELL\vSHELL\v
.context CHDIR
.context MKDIR
.context RMDIR
.context FILES
.topic CHDIR, MKDIR, RMDIR, FILES Statements
CHDIR changes a drive's current directory.  MKDIR makes a directory and
RMDIR removes an empty one.  FILES lists files on the screen.

\bCHDIR\p pathspec$
\bMKDIR\p pathspec$
\bRMDIR\p pathspec$
\bFILES\p [filespec$]

    þ pathspec$   A directory, with a drive letter if the directory is
                  not on the current drive.
    þ filespec$   The files to list: a name or a path, with * and ?
                  allowed.  Without filespec$, every file in the
                  current directory.

CHDIR changes the current directory of the drive named, but does not
make that drive the current one.

FILES shows the directory, then the names in columns (<DIR> after a
directory), then the bytes free on the drive.  If nothing matches, the
error is "File not found".

A directory, or a path to one, that does not exist is "Path not found".
MKDIR of a directory that exists, or RMDIR of one that still has files
in it, is "Path/File access error".

Example:
    MKDIR "C:\\WORK"
    CHDIR "C:\\WORK"
    FILES "*.BAS"
    CHDIR "C:\\"
    RMDIR "C:\\WORK"

See Also  \aKILL\vKILL\v  \aNAME\vNAME\v  \aSHELL\vSHELL\v
.context CHR$
.topic CHR$ Function
CHR$ gives a string of one character, the one with the code given.

\bCHR$\p(code%)

    þ code%   A character code, 0 to 255.

Any other code is "Illegal function call".  CHR$ gives the characters
that cannot be typed into a string in quotation marks, such as the
quotation mark itself, CHR$(34).

Printed on the screen, codes 7, 9 to 13 and 28 to 31 are acted on instead
of shown: the bell, tab, new line, home, clear, and the cursor moves.

Example:
    PRINT CHR$(65); CHR$(66)          ' AB
    PRINT CHR$(34); "quoted"; CHR$(34)

See Also  \aASC\vASC\v  \aSTRING$\vSTRING$\v  \aASCII Character Codes\vascii\v
.context CINT
.topic CINT Function
CINT rounds a number to the nearest whole number and gives the result as
an INTEGER.

\bCINT\p(numeric-expression)

    þ numeric-expression   A number that rounds to -32768 to 32767.

A number halfway between two whole numbers goes to the even one: CINT(2.5)
is 2 and CINT(3.5) is 4.  A number out of range is "Overflow".  INT and
FIX do not round: they cut the fraction off.

Example:
    PRINT CINT(2.5); CINT(2.51); CINT(-2.7)     ' 2  3 -3

See Also  \aCLNG\vCLNG\v  \aINT\vINT\v  \aFIX\vFIX\v  \aCSNG\vCSNG\v  \aCDBL\vCDBL\v
.context CIRCLE
.topic CIRCLE Statement
CIRCLE draws a circle or an ellipse, or an arc of one, in a graphics
mode.

\bCIRCLE\p [STEP] (x!,y!), radius! [,[color%] [,[start!] [,[end!] [,aspect!]]]]

    þ STEP           x! and y! are counted from the last point drawn.
    þ x!, y!         The centre.
    þ radius!        The radius, in the units of the x axis.
    þ color%         The colour.  Left out: the foreground colour.
    þ start!, end!   Where an arc starts and ends: angles in radians,
                     -2*pi to 2*pi, counted anticlockwise from
                     3 o'clock.  Without them, the whole figure.
    þ aspect!        The figure's height over its width.

A negative start! or end! also draws a line from the centre to that end
of the arc, so two negative angles draw a slice of a pie.  The arc itself
is drawn as if the angle were positive.

Without aspect!, the figure looks round on a 4:3 screen: the aspect is
4/3 of the mode's height over its width (.8333 in SCREEN 13).  When
aspect! is below 1, radius! is the x radius; above 1, the y radius.

After CIRCLE, the last point is the centre.  A negative radius, an angle
beyond 2*pi, or CIRCLE in SCREEN 0 is "Illegal function call".

Example:
    SCREEN 12
    CIRCLE (320, 240), 100, 14                 ' a yellow circle
    CIRCLE (320, 240), 100, 12, , , .5         ' a flat red ellipse
    CONST PI = 3.141593
    CIRCLE (160, 100), 60, 10, -PI / 4, -PI / 2    ' a slice

See Also  \aLINE\vLINE\v  \aPAINT\vPAINT\v  \aDRAW\vDRAW\v  \aWINDOW\vWINDOW\v  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v
.context CLEAR
.topic CLEAR Statement
CLEAR closes every file and sets every variable back as it was when the
program started.

\bCLEAR\p [,,stack&]

    þ stack&   In QBasic, the size of the stack, in bytes.

After CLEAR, numbers are 0 and strings are empty; static arrays keep
their size with every element 0 or "", and dynamic arrays are erased.
GOSUB return addresses are forgotten as well, so CLEAR belongs in the
module-level code and not in a subroutine.

In this QBasic, the stack size is accepted and ignored.

Example:
    CLEAR                             ' a fresh start
    CLEAR , , 4096                    ' QBasic: a 4K stack

See Also  \aERASE\vERASE\v  \aCLOSE\vCLOSE\v  \aRUN\vRUN\v  \aFRE\vFRE\v
.context CLNG
.topic CLNG Function
CLNG rounds a number to the nearest whole number and gives the result as
a LONG.

\bCLNG\p(numeric-expression)

    þ numeric-expression   A number that rounds to -2147483648 to
                           2147483647.

A number halfway between two whole numbers goes to the even one: CLNG(2.5)
is 2 and CLNG(3.5) is 4.  A number out of range is "Overflow".

Example:
    bytes& = CLNG(kb! * 1024)
    PRINT CLNG(123456.5)              ' 123456

See Also  \aCINT\vCINT\v  \aINT\vINT\v  \aFIX\vFIX\v  \aCSNG\vCSNG\v  \aCDBL\vCDBL\v
.context CLOSE
.topic CLOSE Statement
CLOSE ends the use of files and devices opened with OPEN.

\bCLOSE\p [#filenumber% [, #filenumber%]...]

    þ filenumber%   The number the file was opened under.  The # may
                    be left out.

CLOSE with no numbers closes every open file and device.  What is still
waiting in a file's buffer is written out first.  Closing a number that
is not open is not an error, but a number outside 1 to 255 is "Bad file
name or number".

END, SYSTEM, RESET, CLEAR and RUN close all files too.

Example:
    OPEN "LOG.TXT" FOR APPEND AS #1
    PRINT #1, DATE$; " "; TIME$
    CLOSE #1

See Also  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v  \aRESET\vRESET\v  \aEND\vEND\v  \aFREEFILE\vFREEFILE\v
.context CLS
.topic CLS Statement
CLS clears the screen, or part of it.

\bCLS\p [{0 | 1 | 2}]

    þ 0      The whole screen, text and graphics.
    þ 1      The graphics viewport, or the whole picture if VIEW has
             not set one.  In SCREEN 0, nothing is cleared.
    þ 2      The text viewport: VIEW PRINT's rows.
    þ none   The graphics viewport if VIEW set one, else the text
             viewport if VIEW PRINT set one, else the whole screen.

The cleared part takes the background colour.  After CLS 0 or CLS 2,
the cursor is at the top left of the text viewport.  In a graphics mode,
CLS 0 or 1 also makes the centre of the viewport the last point drawn.
Any other number is "Illegal function call".

Example:
    COLOR 15, 1: CLS                  ' white on blue
    VIEW PRINT 5 TO 20: CLS 2         ' rows 5 to 20 only

See Also  \aVIEW\vVIEW\v  \aVIEW PRINT\vVIEW-PRINT\v  \aCOLOR\vCOLOR\v  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v
.context COLOR
.topic COLOR Statement
COLOR sets the colours PRINT and the drawing statements use.  What the
numbers mean depends on the screen mode.

\bCOLOR\p [foreground%] [, [background%] [, border%]]    SCREEN 0
\bCOLOR\p [background%] [, palette%]                      SCREEN 1
\bCOLOR\p [foreground%] [, background&]                   SCREEN 7 to 10
\bCOLOR\p [foreground%]                                   SCREEN 12, 13

    þ foreground%   The colour of text, and of drawing that names no
                    colour of its own.
    þ background%   SCREEN 0: the colour behind the characters printed
                    from now on, 0 to 7.  SCREEN 1: the colour of the
                    whole background, 0 to 15.
    þ background&   SCREEN 7 to 10: the colour attribute 0 shows, which
                    is the whole background.
    þ border%       SCREEN 0: the colour round the edge, 0 to 15.
    þ palette%      SCREEN 1: colours 1 to 3 become green, red and yellow
                    (0) or cyan, magenta and white (1).

In SCREEN 0, foreground% is 0 to 15; add 16 to make the text blink.  A
CLS after COLOR fills the screen with the new background.

    0 black     4 red        8 grey          12 light red
    1 blue      5 magenta    9 light blue    13 light magenta
    2 green     6 brown     10 light green   14 yellow
    3 cyan      7 white     11 light cyan    15 bright white

In SCREEN 7 to 13, foreground% is an attribute (0 to 15; 0 to 3 in
SCREEN 10, 0 to 255 in SCREEN 13), and PALETTE sets the colour each
attribute shows.  COLOR in SCREEN 2 or 11, or a number out of range, is
"Illegal function call".

In this QBasic, the border colour is accepted but not shown.

Example:
    COLOR 14, 1: CLS                  ' yellow on blue
    PRINT "Warning": COLOR 7, 0
    COLOR 31: PRINT "Blinking"

See Also  \aPALETTE\vPALETTE\v  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v  \aCLS\vCLS\v  \aPRINT\vPRINT\v
.context COM
.topic COM, ON COM Statements
ON COM names a subroutine to run when characters arrive at a serial
port.  COM(n%) turns the trap on or off, or holds the trap.

\bON COM\p(n%) \bGOSUB\p line
\bCOM\p(n%) {ON | OFF | STOP}

    þ n%     The port: 1 for COM1, 2 for COM2.
    þ line   The label or line number where the subroutine starts.
             0 removes the subroutine.
    þ ON     A character arriving runs the subroutine, between two
             statements.
    þ OFF    Characters arriving are not noticed.
    þ STOP   A character arriving is noted, and the subroutine runs
             at the next COM(n%) ON.

The subroutine ends with RETURN.  While the subroutine runs, the port's
trap is held as by STOP, so the subroutine does not start again inside
itself.

In this QBasic, COM events never happen: the statements are accepted,
but the subroutine is not called.

Example:
    OPEN "COM1:2400,N,8,1" FOR RANDOM AS #1
    ON COM(1) GOSUB GotChar
    COM(1) ON

See Also  \aEvent Trapping\vevents\v  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v  \aGOSUB\vGOSUB\v
.context COMMON
.topic COMMON Statement
COMMON names variables that a program hands on to the program it CHAINs
to.  With SHARED, the module's SUBs and FUNCTIONs can use them too.

\bCOMMON\p [SHARED] variablelist

    þ SHARED         Every procedure in the module can use the
                     variables, as after DIM SHARED.
    þ variablelist   Variables, with commas between them, each written
                     name[()] [AS type].  An array is written with empty
                     parentheses, and given its size by a DIM after the
                     COMMON.

Put COMMON at the top of the module, before the program's statements.
The two programs' COMMON lists are matched by position and type, not by
name.

A value whose variable in the other program is of another type is not
passed on.

Example:
    COMMON SHARED total, names$()
    DIM names$(50)
    total = 7
    ShowTotal                         ' a SUB that reads total

See Also  \aCHAIN\vCHAIN\v  \aSHARED\vSHARED\v  \aDIM\vDIM\v
.context CONST
.topic CONST Statement
CONST gives a name to a value that does not change while the program
runs.

\bCONST\p name = expression [, name = expression]...

    þ name         The constant's name.  A type suffix (% & ! # $)
                   sets its type; without one, the value's type does.
    þ expression   Numbers, strings, other constants and operators.
                   Variables and functions are not allowed.

A constant cannot be assigned to.  A CONST in the module-level code can
be used everywhere in the module, its procedures too; a CONST in a SUB or
FUNCTION only in that procedure.  DEFtype statements do not change a
constant's type.

Example:
    CONST PI = 3.141593, MAXROWS% = 25
    CONST TITLE$ = "Inventory"
    CONST AREA = PI * 4

See Also  \aDIM\vDIM\v  \aData Types\vtypes\v
.context COS
.topic COS Function
COS gives the cosine of an angle in radians.

\bCOS\p(angle)

    þ angle   The angle, in radians.

The result is a DOUBLE when the argument is a DOUBLE, otherwise a SINGLE.
For an angle in degrees, multiply the degrees by pi / 180 first.

Example:
    CONST PI = 3.141593
    PRINT COS(0); COS(PI)             ' 1 -1
    x = cx + r * COS(a): y = cy - r * SIN(a)

See Also  \aSIN\vSIN\v  \aTAN\vTAN\v  \aATN\vATN\v
.context CSNG
.topic CSNG Function
CSNG gives a number as a SINGLE (single precision).

\bCSNG\p(numeric-expression)

    þ numeric-expression   Any number within the range of a SINGLE.

A DOUBLE is rounded to the 7 or so digits a SINGLE holds.  A DOUBLE
beyond the range of a SINGLE (about 3.4E+38) is "Overflow".

Example:
    d# = 1 / 3#
    PRINT d#, CSNG(d#)                ' .3333333333333333  .3333333

See Also  \aCDBL\vCDBL\v  \aCINT\vCINT\v  \aCLNG\vCLNG\v  \aData Types\vtypes\v
.context CSRLIN
.topic CSRLIN Function
CSRLIN gives the row the cursor is on, counted from 1 at the top.  POS(0)
gives its column.

\bCSRLIN\p

Example:
    row = CSRLIN: col = POS(0)
    LOCATE 25, 1: PRINT "Working...";
    LOCATE row, col                   ' back where the cursor was

See Also  \aPOS\vPOS\v  \aLOCATE\vLOCATE\v
.context CVI
.context CVL
.context CVS
.context CVD
.topic CVI, CVL, CVS, CVD Functions
These turn a string of bytes, as MKI$, MKL$, MKS$ or MKD$ made the
string, back into a number.  They read the numbers kept in random-access
files.

\bCVI\p(2-byte-string$)
\bCVL\p(4-byte-string$)
\bCVS\p(4-byte-string$)
\bCVD\p(8-byte-string$)

CVI gives an INTEGER, CVL a LONG, CVS a SINGLE and CVD a DOUBLE.  A
string shorter than that is "Illegal function call"; bytes after those
needed are not used.

CVS and CVD read the IEEE format QBasic keeps numbers in.  For files
written by GW-BASIC or an older QuickBASIC, use CVSMBF and CVDMBF, or
start QBasic with /MBF, which makes CVS and CVD read that format.

Example:
    FIELD #1, 2 AS id$, 8 AS price$
    GET #1, 5
    PRINT CVI(id$), CVD(price$)

See Also  \aMKI$\vMKI$\v  \aCVSMBF\vCVSMBF\v  \aFIELD\vFIELD\v  \aGET\vGET\v
.context CVSMBF
.context CVDMBF
.topic CVSMBF, CVDMBF Functions
CVSMBF and CVDMBF read numbers kept in Microsoft Binary Format, the
floating point of GW-BASIC and of QuickBASIC before version 4.0.

\bCVSMBF\p(4-byte-string$)
\bCVDMBF\p(8-byte-string$)

CVSMBF gives a SINGLE and CVDMBF a DOUBLE.  A string that is too short is
"Illegal function call".  They are for reading the random-access files
that older programs wrote; MKSMBF$ and MKDMBF$ make strings in the same
format.

Example:
    FIELD #1, 4 AS amount$
    GET #1, rec&
    PRINT CVSMBF(amount$)

See Also  \aMKSMBF$\vMKSMBF$\v  \aCVI\vCVI\v  \aFIELD\vFIELD\v
.context DATA
.topic DATA Statement
DATA holds numbers and strings for READ to take, one after another.

\bDATA\p constant [, constant]...

    þ constant   A number or a string.  A string needs quotation marks
                 only if the string holds a comma or a colon, or has
                 blanks at either end that must be kept.

READ takes the items in order, from the first DATA statement in the
program to the last; RESTORE starts them again.  The program passes over
DATA without doing anything, so DATA can go anywhere in the module-level
code.  A DATA statement ends at a colon that is not in quotation marks.

An empty item reads as 0 or "".  An item read into a numeric variable
that is not a number is "Syntax error", and a READ after the last item
is "Out of DATA".

Example:
    FOR i = 1 TO 3
        READ who$, age
        PRINT who$; age
    NEXT
    DATA Ann, 34, "Smith, Bob", 51, Cy, 8

See Also  \aREAD\vREAD\v  \aRESTORE\vRESTORE\v
.context DATE$
.topic DATE$ Function and Statement
The DATE$ function gives the computer's date as a string.  The DATE$
statement sets the date.

\bDATE$\p
\bDATE$\p = stringexpression$

    þ stringexpression$   The new date, as mm-dd-yy, mm-dd-yyyy,
                          mm/dd/yy or mm/dd/yyyy.

The function's answer is always mm-dd-yyyy: "10-25-2026".  The year must
be from 1980 to 2099.  A two-digit year from 80 to 99 means 19yy; in this
QBasic, one below 80 means 20yy.  A date that is not valid is "Illegal
function call".

Example:
    PRINT "Today is "; DATE$
    yr = VAL(RIGHT$(DATE$, 4))
    DATE$ = "01-15-2027"

See Also  \aTIME$\vTIME$\v  \aTIMER\vTIMER\v
.context DECLARE
.topic DECLARE Statement
DECLARE names a SUB or FUNCTION and its parameters, so that every call to
the procedure can be checked against them.

\bDECLARE\p {SUB | FUNCTION} name [([parameterlist])]

    þ name            The procedure's name.  A FUNCTION's type suffix
                      goes on its name.
    þ parameterlist   The parameters, with commas between them, each
                      written name[()] [AS type].  AS ANY takes an
                      argument of any type, with no type check.

When QBasic saves a program, QBasic puts a DECLARE for each procedure
that lacks one at the top of the module, so DECLAREs seldom need typing.
A DECLARE that does not agree with its procedure is "Argument-count
mismatch" or "Parameter type mismatch".

Example:
    DECLARE SUB Box (x%, y%, w%, h%)
    DECLARE FUNCTION Average! (list!(), n%)

See Also  \aSUB\vSUB\v  \aFUNCTION\vFUNCTION\v  \aCALL\vCALL\v  \aData Types\vtypes\v
.context DEF
.topic DEF FN Statement
DEF FN makes a function in the module-level code.  The function's name
starts with FN.

\bDEF FN\pname[(parameterlist)] = expression

\bDEF FN\pname[(parameterlist)]
    [statements]
    FNname = expression
    [statements]
\bEND DEF\p

    þ name            The name after FN.  A type suffix on the name
                      gives the type of the value.
    þ parameterlist   Variables, with commas between them, each
                      written name [AS type].
    þ expression      The value the function returns.

The parameters are the function's own and get copies of the arguments.
Every other variable in a DEF FN is the module's.  In the many-line form
the value is assigned to FNname, and EXIT DEF returns at once.

A DEF FN must come earlier in the program than the first use of the
function, or the use is "Function not defined".  DEF FN inside a SUB or
FUNCTION is "Illegal in procedure".  A FUNCTION procedure does the same
job with its own variables.

Example:
    DEF FNarea (r) = 3.141593 * r * r
    PRINT FNarea(2)                   ' 12.56637
    DEF FNmax (a, b)
        IF a > b THEN FNmax = a ELSE FNmax = b
    END DEF

See Also  \aFUNCTION\vFUNCTION\v  \aEXIT\vEXIT\v  \aSTATIC\vSTATIC\v
.context SEG
.topic DEF SEG Statement
DEF SEG sets the segment that PEEK, POKE, BLOAD, BSAVE and CALL ABSOLUTE
work in.

\bDEF SEG\p [= address]

    þ address   A segment, 0 to 65535 (&HFFFF).  Without an address,
                the segment is QBasic's own data, where the program's
                variables are.

The segment stays set until the next DEF SEG.  An address outside -32768
to 65535 is "Overflow".

In this QBasic there is no real mode, and DEF SEG reaches only the
program's own variables (the segments VARSEG and SADD give), the screen
at &HB800 and &HA000, and the BIOS data area at &H40 and the ROMs, which
can be read but not changed.  Anything else reads as 0 and POKE there
does nothing.

Example:
    DEF SEG = &HB800
    POKE 0, 65: POKE 1, &H1E          ' a yellow A, top left
    DEF SEG                           ' back to QBasic's data

See Also  \aPEEK\vPEEK\v  \aPOKE\vPOKE\v  \aVARSEG\vVARSEG\v  \aBLOAD\vBLOAD\v  \aQBasic on PM-DOS\vpmdos\v
.context DEFINT
.context DEFLNG
.context DEFSNG
.context DEFDBL
.context DEFSTR
.topic DEFtype Statements
The DEFtype statements give a type, by the first letter of the name, to
the variables, arrays and functions whose names have no type suffix and
no AS.

\bDEFINT\p letterrange [, letterrange]...
\bDEFLNG\p letterrange [, letterrange]...
\bDEFSNG\p letterrange [, letterrange]...
\bDEFDBL\p letterrange [, letterrange]...
\bDEFSTR\p letterrange [, letterrange]...

    þ letterrange   A letter, or two letters with a minus between them
                    (A-Z) for those letters and every one in between.

DEFINT gives INTEGER, DEFLNG LONG, DEFSNG SINGLE, DEFDBL DOUBLE and
DEFSTR STRING.  Without a DEFtype statement, names are SINGLE.  A suffix
(% & ! # $) or an AS always counts first.

A DEFtype statement holds from where the statement is to the end of its
module or procedure.  When a SUB or FUNCTION is made, QBasic copies the
module's DEFtype statements to the top of the new procedure.

Example:
    DEFINT A-Z                        ' every name INTEGER...
    DEFSTR S                          ' ...but those starting with S
    count = 10: s = "ten"

See Also  \aData Types\vtypes\v  \aDIM\vDIM\v
.context DIM
.context REDIM
.topic DIM, REDIM Statements
DIM declares variables and arrays and gives arrays their size.  REDIM
gives a dynamic array a new size, and empties the array.

\bDIM\p [SHARED] variable[(subscripts)] [AS type] [, variable...]
\bREDIM\p [SHARED] variable(subscripts) [AS type] [, variable...]

    þ SHARED       In the module-level code: every SUB and FUNCTION of
                   the module can use the variable too.
    þ variable     A name, with a type suffix if wanted.
    þ subscripts   One range for each dimension, with commas between,
                   each [lower TO] upper.  Without lower, the lowest
                   subscript is 0, or 1 after OPTION BASE 1.
    þ AS type      INTEGER, LONG, SINGLE, DOUBLE, STRING, STRING * n
                   (n characters, always), or a TYPE.

An array whose subscripts are all constants is static: it is made before
the program starts and keeps its size.  An array with a variable in its
subscripts, one after $DYNAMIC, or one made by REDIM is dynamic: it is
made when the statement runs, and ERASE or REDIM can free it.

Every element starts as 0 or "".  An array used without a DIM gets
subscripts up to 10.  A second DIM of a static array is "Array already
dimensioned"; of a dynamic one, "Duplicate definition" when the DIM
runs.  A subscript outside the bounds is "Subscript out of range".  An
array may have up to 60 dimensions.

In QBasic, an array holds at most 64K; in this QBasic an array can be
far bigger.

Example:
    DIM total AS LONG, title AS STRING * 20
    DIM grid(1 TO 10, 1 TO 10) AS INTEGER
    DIM SHARED names$(100)
    n = 50: REDIM scores(1 TO n)

See Also  \aERASE\vERASE\v  \aLBOUND\vLBOUND\v  \aUBOUND\vUBOUND\v  \aOPTION BASE\vOPTION\v  \a$DYNAMIC\vmeta\v  \aTYPE\vTYPE\v
.context DO
.context LOOP
.context UNTIL
.topic DO...LOOP Statement
DO...LOOP runs a block of statements again and again, while a condition
is true or until it becomes true.

\bDO\p [{WHILE | UNTIL} condition]
    [statements]
\bLOOP\p

\bDO\p
    [statements]
\bLOOP\p [{WHILE | UNTIL} condition]

    þ condition   A numeric expression: true if it is not 0, false if
                  it is 0.
    þ WHILE       The block runs again while the condition is true.
    þ UNTIL       The block runs again until the condition is true.

With the test after DO, the block may not run at all; with the test after
LOOP, the block always runs at least once.  With no test, the loop goes
on until EXIT DO or a GOTO leaves the loop.  Ctrl+Break stops a loop
that never ends.

Example:
    DO WHILE NOT EOF(1)
        LINE INPUT #1, l$: PRINT l$
    LOOP
    DO
        k$ = INKEY$
    LOOP UNTIL k$ <> ""

See Also  \aWHILE...WEND\vWHILE\v  \aFOR...NEXT\vFOR\v  \aEXIT\vEXIT\v
.context DRAW
.topic DRAW Statement
DRAW draws lines from a string of short commands, in a graphics mode.
Each move starts at the last point drawn.

\bDRAW\p commandstring$

Moves (n is a distance; 1 if left out):
    U n    up                     E n    up and right
    D n    down                   F n    down and right
    L n    left                   G n    down and left
    R n    right                  H n    up and left
    M x,y  to the point (x, y)
    M +x,y or M -x,y  by x across and y down (a sign on x makes M
           relative)

Before a move:
    B      Move without drawing.
    N      Draw, then come back to where the move started.

Other commands:
    A n    Turns the moves that follow by n * 90 degrees anticlockwise
           (n is 0 to 3).
    TA n   Turns the moves that follow by n degrees, -360 to 360;
           positive is anticlockwise.
    C n    Draws in colour n.
    S n    Scale: a distance of 1 is n/4 pixels (n is 1 to 255; 4 to
           start with).
    P p,b  Paints the area round the last point in colour p, up to the
           border colour b.
    X s    Runs the commands in another string: "X" + VARPTR$(s$).

A number can also be a variable's value, as "=" + VARPTR$(variable), or
be joined in with STR$.  Blanks and semicolons between commands do not
matter, and small letters do as well as capitals.  A, TA and S stay set
for the DRAW statements that follow.

An unknown command or a number out of range is "Illegal function call",
as is DRAW in SCREEN 0.

Example:
    SCREEN 12
    DRAW "BM100,100 C14 R50 D50 L50 U50"      ' a yellow square
    DRAW "BM200,100 TA45 R40 TA0"             ' a line at 45 degrees
    tri$ = "E30 F30 L60"
    DRAW "BM300,200 X" + VARPTR$(tri$)
    d = 25: DRAW "R" + STR$(d)

See Also  \aLINE\vLINE\v  \aPSET\vPSET\v  \aPAINT\vPAINT\v  \aVARPTR$\vVARPTR$\v  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v
.context END
.topic END Statement
END on its own ends the program.  END with a keyword closes a block.

\bEND\p
\bEND\p {DEF | FUNCTION | IF | SELECT | SUB | TYPE}

    þ DEF        Ends a DEF FN of many lines.
    þ FUNCTION   Ends a FUNCTION procedure.
    þ IF         Ends a block IF.
    þ SELECT     Ends a SELECT CASE.
    þ SUB        Ends a SUB procedure.
    þ TYPE       Ends a TYPE definition.

END closes every file.  A program that runs past its last statement ends
in the same way.  In the editor, "Press any key to continue" is shown
under the program's output, and the variables keep their values for the
Immediate window.  After QBASIC /RUN, SYSTEM ends the program and QBasic
together.

Example:
    INPUT "Again (Y/N)"; a$
    IF UCASE$(a$) <> "Y" THEN END

See Also  \aSYSTEM\vSYSTEM\v  \aSTOP\vSTOP\v  \aEXIT\vEXIT\v
.context ENVIRON
.context ENVIRON$
.topic ENVIRON Statement, ENVIRON$ Function
ENVIRON$ reads a variable of the DOS environment, such as PATH.  ENVIRON
sets or removes one in QBasic's copy of the environment.

\bENVIRON$\p(name$)
\bENVIRON$\p(n%)
\bENVIRON\p stringexpression$

    þ name$               The variable's name.  Names are matched as
                          written, so ask for "PATH", not "path".
    þ n%                  The n'th entry, 1 to 255, given whole:
                          "PATH=C:\\DOS".
    þ stringexpression$   "name=value" (or "name value").  With nothing
                          after the =, the variable is removed.

ENVIRON$ gives "" for a name that is not set, or for an n past the last
entry.  An empty name, a name with a blank or = in it, or an n outside
1 to 255 is "Illegal function call".

The changes last until QBasic ends, and a program started by SHELL gets
them; the environment of the DOS that started QBasic is not changed.

Example:
    PRINT ENVIRON$("PATH")
    ENVIRON "TEMP=C:\\TMP"
    i = 1
    DO WHILE ENVIRON$(i) <> ""
        PRINT ENVIRON$(i): i = i + 1
    LOOP

See Also  \aSHELL\vSHELL\v
.context EOF
.topic EOF Function
EOF tells whether a file has been read to its end: -1 (true) at the end,
0 (false) before the end.

\bEOF\p(filenumber%)

    þ filenumber%   The number of an open file.

A file opened FOR INPUT is at its end when nothing is left to read; a
Ctrl+Z (CHR$(26)) in it counts as the end too.  A random-access or binary
file is at its end once a GET has tried to read past the last record or
byte.  Testing EOF before each INPUT # keeps the program from stopping
with "Input past end of file".

Example:
    OPEN "NAMES.TXT" FOR INPUT AS #1
    DO UNTIL EOF(1)
        LINE INPUT #1, l$
        PRINT l$
    LOOP
    CLOSE #1

See Also  \aLOF\vLOF\v  \aLOC\vLOC\v  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v  \aINPUT\vINPUT\v  \aLINE INPUT\vLINE-INPUT\v
.context ERASE
.topic ERASE Statement
ERASE empties arrays.

\bERASE\p arrayname [, arrayname]...

    þ arrayname   An array's name, without parentheses.

A static array keeps its size, and each element goes back to 0 or "".  A
dynamic array is freed: DIM or REDIM must give the array a size again,
and until then using the array is "Subscript out of range".

Example:
    DIM a(100)
    REDIM b$(n)
    ERASE a, b$                       ' a() is zeros, b$() is gone

See Also  \aDIM\vDIM\v  \aREDIM\vREDIM\v  \aCLEAR\vCLEAR\v  \a$DYNAMIC\vmeta\v
.context ERDEV
.context ERDEV$
.topic ERDEV, ERDEV$ Functions
After a device fails, ERDEV gives a code for the error and ERDEV$ the
name of the device.

\bERDEV\p
\bERDEV$\p

In QBasic, the low byte of ERDEV is the error number DOS gave for the
device (the critical-error code), and the high byte holds bits of the
device's attribute word.  ERDEV$ is a device's name, such as "LPT1", or a
drive's, such as "A:".  An ON ERROR routine reads them.

In this QBasic, ERDEV is always 0 and ERDEV$ is always "".

Example:
    PRINT "Error"; ERR; "on "; ERDEV$; ", code"; ERDEV

See Also  \aERR\vERR\v  \aON ERROR\vON-ERROR\v
.context ERR
.context ERL
.topic ERR, ERL Functions
In an error routine, ERR gives the code of the error and ERL the number
of the line the error happened on.

\bERR\p
\bERL\p

ERR is 11 for "Division by zero", 53 for "File not found", and so on;
RESUME sets ERR back to 0.  ERL is the number of the line that failed, or
of the nearest numbered line above it.  Labels that are words do not
count, and ERL is 0 if there is no numbered line before the error.

Example:
    ON ERROR GOTO Handler
    OPEN "DATA.TXT" FOR INPUT AS #1
    END
    Handler:
    PRINT "Error"; ERR; "in line"; ERL
    RESUME NEXT

See Also  \aON ERROR\vON-ERROR\v  \aRESUME\vRESUME\v  \aERROR\vERROR\v  \aRun-Time Error Codes\verrcodes\v
.context ERROR
.topic ERROR Statement
ERROR makes an error happen, as if a statement had failed: to test an
error routine, or to report an error of the program's own.

\bERROR\p n%

    þ n%   The error's code, 1 to 255.

With ON ERROR in force, ERROR goes to the error routine with ERR set to
n%.  Without ON ERROR, the program stops with the message for n%, or with
"Unprintable error" for a code QBasic has no message for.  A code of 0,
or over 255, is "Illegal function call".  Codes QBasic does not use,
such as 200 and up, are best for the program's own errors.

Example:
    ON ERROR GOTO Trouble
    IF qty < 0 THEN ERROR 200
    END
    Trouble:
    IF ERR = 200 THEN PRINT "The quantity is below zero": RESUME NEXT

See Also  \aERR\vERR\v  \aON ERROR\vON-ERROR\v  \aRESUME\vRESUME\v  \aRun-Time Error Codes\verrcodes\v
.context EXIT
.topic EXIT Statement
EXIT leaves a loop, a procedure or a DEF FN at once.

\bEXIT\p {DEF | DO | FOR | FUNCTION | SUB}

    þ DEF        Returns from the DEF FN that is running.
    þ DO         Goes on after the LOOP of the innermost DO...LOOP.
    þ FOR        Goes on after the NEXT of the innermost FOR...NEXT.
    þ FUNCTION   Returns from the FUNCTION that is running.
    þ SUB        Returns from the SUB that is running.

A FUNCTION or DEF FN left by EXIT returns the value last assigned to its
name, or 0 or "" if none was.  EXIT DO outside a DO...LOOP is "EXIT DO
not within DO...LOOP", and the other forms have messages like that one.

Example:
    FOR i = 1 TO LEN(s$)
        IF MID$(s$, i, 1) = " " THEN EXIT FOR
    NEXT
    PRINT "First blank at"; i

See Also  \aDO...LOOP\vDO\v  \aFOR...NEXT\vFOR\v  \aSUB\vSUB\v  \aFUNCTION\vFUNCTION\v  \aDEF FN\vDEF\v
.context EXP
.topic EXP Function
EXP gives e (2.718282) raised to a power.  EXP is the inverse of LOG.

\bEXP\p(x)

    þ x   The power: up to about 88.72 for a SINGLE result, up to
          709.78 for a DOUBLE.

The result is a DOUBLE when x is a DOUBLE, otherwise a SINGLE.  A power
too large is "Overflow"; a large negative one gives 0.

Example:
    PRINT EXP(1)                      ' 2.718282
    PRINT EXP(1#)                     ' 2.718281828459045
    growth = start * EXP(rate * years)

See Also  \aLOG\vLOG\v  \aArithmetic Operators\varith\v
.context FIELD
.topic FIELD Statement
FIELD divides the record buffer of a file opened FOR RANDOM into string
variables.  GET fills them from the file and PUT writes what they hold.

\bFIELD\p [#]filenumber%, width% \bAS\p var$ [, width% \bAS\p var$]...

    þ filenumber%   The number of a file open FOR RANDOM.
    þ width%        How many bytes of the record the variable takes.
    þ var$          The string variable that stands for those bytes.

The fields follow one another from the start of the record.  Their widths
added up must not pass the record length (LEN = in OPEN), or the error is
"FIELD overflow".  A file not open FOR RANDOM gives "Bad file mode".

Text goes into a field variable with LSET or RSET, which keep its width
and fill the buffer too.  A LET or INPUT into the variable changes the
variable but not the buffer.  Numbers go in as MKI$, MKL$, MKS$ or MKD$
strings and come out with CVI, CVL, CVS or CVD.  A record variable (see
TYPE) with GET and PUT does the same work with less code.

A second FIELD for the same file adds to the first: the variables of
both stand for parts of the one record.

Example:
    OPEN "PHONES.DAT" FOR RANDOM AS #1 LEN = 30
    FIELD #1, 20 AS person$, 10 AS phone$
    LSET person$ = "Ann Smith"
    LSET phone$ = "555-0123"
    PUT #1, 1
    CLOSE #1

See Also  \aGET, PUT\vGET\v  \aLSET\vLSET\v  \aMKI$\vMKI$\v  \aCVI\vCVI\v  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v  \aTYPE\vTYPE\v
.context FILEATTR
.topic FILEATTR Function
FILEATTR tells how a file was opened, or gives the DOS handle behind its
file number.

\bFILEATTR\p(filenumber%, attribute%)

    þ filenumber%   The number of an open file.
    þ attribute%    1 for the mode, 2 for the DOS file handle.

For attribute% 1 the answer is:

    1  INPUT      2  OUTPUT     4  RANDOM     8  APPEND    32  BINARY

Any other attribute% gives "Illegal function call", and a number with no
file open on it gives "Bad file name or number".  For attribute% 2, the
screen and keyboard devices (SCRN:, KYBD:, CONS:) give 0.

Example:
    OPEN "LOG.TXT" FOR APPEND AS #2
    IF FILEATTR(2, 1) = 8 THEN PRINT "Adding to the end"

See Also  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v  \aFREEFILE\vFREEFILE\v
.context FIX
.topic FIX Function
FIX drops the fraction from a number, so the result moves toward zero.

\bFIX\p(numeric-expression)

FIX(2.8) is 2 and FIX(-2.8) is -2.  INT goes down instead: INT(-2.8) is
-3.  CINT rounds to the nearest whole number.  The result has the type of
the argument.

Example:
    PRINT FIX(7.9), FIX(-7.9)        ' 7  -7
    PRINT INT(7.9), INT(-7.9)        ' 7  -8

See Also  \aINT\vINT\v  \aCINT\vCINT\v  \aCLNG\vCLNG\v
.context FOR
.context NEXT
.context TO
.context STEP
.topic FOR...NEXT Statement
FOR...NEXT runs a group of statements a set number of times, stepping a
counter from a first value to a last one.

\bFOR\p counter = start \bTO\p end [\bSTEP\p increment]
    [statements]
\bNEXT\p [counter [, counter]...]

    þ counter      A numeric variable; not an array element or a
                   record's element.
    þ start, end   The first value, and the value the counter must not
                   pass.
    þ increment    Added at each NEXT; 1 when STEP is left out.  A
                   negative increment counts down.

end and increment are worked out once, when FOR starts.  The test comes
first: when start is already past end, the statements do not run at all.
After the last pass the counter holds the first value past end.  With
STEP 0 the loop never ends, unless start is past end.

NEXT alone closes the nearest FOR; NEXT j, i closes two loops at once.
EXIT FOR leaves early.  A counter that would step outside its type gives
"Overflow", so FOR i% = 1 TO 32767 fails at its last NEXT.

TO also marks a range in DIM, SELECT CASE and LOCK.  STEP also makes a
point relative to the last one in the graphics statements.

Example:
    FOR i = 10 TO 1 STEP -3
        PRINT i;
    NEXT i                           ' 10  7  4  1

See Also  \aDO...LOOP\vDO\v  \aWHILE...WEND\vWHILE\v  \aEXIT\vEXIT\v
.context FRE
.topic FRE Function
FRE tells how much memory is left.

\bFRE\p(numeric-expression)
\bFRE\p(stringexpression$)

    þ -1          The memory free for arrays and everything else.
    þ -2          The stack space not in use.
    þ any other   The string space free.  A string argument gives the
                  same.

QBasic keeps strings in one 64K segment, and FRE counts what is left of
that.  FRE here counts the same way - at most 65,535 bytes, less the text
the program holds - but strings are not kept in one segment, so they can
go past that figure.  FRE(-1) can be far larger than under MS-DOS.

Example:
    PRINT "Memory free:"; FRE(-1)
    PRINT "String space:"; FRE("")

See Also  \aCLEAR\vCLEAR\v  \aERASE\vERASE\v  \aQBasic Limits\vlimits\v
.context FREEFILE
.topic FREEFILE Function
FREEFILE gives the lowest file number that no open file is using.

\bFREEFILE\p

File numbers run from 1 to 255.  When all of them are in use the error
is "Too many files".  FREEFILE only finds the number; nothing is open on
it until OPEN uses it, so two FREEFILEs before an OPEN give the same
number.

Example:
    handle% = FREEFILE
    OPEN "NOTES.TXT" FOR INPUT AS #handle%
    LINE INPUT #handle%, first$
    CLOSE #handle%

See Also  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v  \aCLOSE\vCLOSE\v  \aFILEATTR\vFILEATTR\v
.context FUNCTION
.topic FUNCTION Statement
FUNCTION starts a procedure that works out a value and hands it back, for
use in expressions.  END FUNCTION closes the procedure.

\bFUNCTION\p name [(parameterlist)] [\bSTATIC\p]
    [statements]
    name = expression
    [statements]
\bEND FUNCTION\p

    þ name            The function's name.  A type suffix (% & ! # $)
                      or the DEFtype in force gives the type of its
                      value.
    þ parameterlist   The variables that take the arguments, with
                      commas between them, each one
                          variable[( )] [\bAS\p type]
                      ( ) marks an array.  type is INTEGER, LONG,
                      SINGLE, DOUBLE, STRING or the name of a TYPE.
    þ STATIC          The function's variables keep their values from
                      one call to the next.

The value handed back is the last one assigned to name; with none, 0 or
"".  EXIT FUNCTION leaves at once.  A function is called by using its name
in an expression, with the arguments in parentheses.

Arguments are passed by reference: a variable given as an argument
changes when the function changes the parameter.  Put the variable in
parentheses, as in Area((side)), to pass a copy.  Without STATIC the
function's own variables start at 0 or "" on every call, and the module's
variables are reached only if they are SHARED.  A function may call
itself; too deep a recursion gives "Out of stack space".

The editor keeps each procedure in a text of its own: Edit > New FUNCTION,
or a FUNCTION line typed in the module, starts one, and View > SUBs (F2)
moves between them.  Saving puts a DECLARE for each procedure at the top
of the module.

Example:
    FUNCTION Larger (first, second)
        Larger = first
        IF second > first THEN Larger = second
    END FUNCTION

See Also  \aSUB\vSUB\v  \aDECLARE\vDECLARE\v  \aDEF FN\vDEF\v  \aSHARED\vSHARED\v  \aSTATIC\vSTATIC\v  \aEXIT\vEXIT\v
.context GET
.context PUT
.topic GET, PUT Statements (File I/O)
GET reads a record from a RANDOM file, or bytes from a BINARY file, into
the FIELD buffer or a variable.  PUT writes one.  For the graphics GET
and PUT, see \aGET, PUT (Graphics)\vGET-graphics\v.

\bGET\p [#]filenumber% [, [position&] [, variable]]
\bPUT\p [#]filenumber% [, [position&] [, variable]]

    þ filenumber%   The number of a file open FOR RANDOM or BINARY.
    þ position&     RANDOM: the record number.  BINARY: the byte
                    position.  Both count from 1.  Left out, the
                    record after the last one read or written, or the
                    current byte.
    þ variable      The variable to read into or write from.  Left out
                    (RANDOM only), the FIELD buffer is used.

In a RANDOM file the variable must fit in the record (LEN = in OPEN), or
the error is "Bad record length".  A variable-length string is put as
two bytes of length and then the text, so the record needs two bytes
more than the text.  PUT of a variable writes only the variable's bytes;
the rest of the record stays as it was.

In a BINARY file, GET into a string reads as many bytes as the string
already holds, and PUT writes the string's text alone.  A variable of any
other type moves the bytes the variable takes in memory: 2 for INTEGER,
4 for LONG and SINGLE, 8 for DOUBLE, a record's whole size.

A GET past the end of the file gives zeros, and EOF becomes true.  A
position below 1 gives "Bad record number"; a file in another mode gives
"Bad file mode".

Example:
    DIM block AS STRING * 16
    OPEN "DATA.BIN" FOR BINARY AS #1
    GET #1, 1, block                 ' the first 16 bytes
    PUT #1, LOF(1) + 1, block        ' copied onto the end
    CLOSE #1

See Also  \aFIELD\vFIELD\v  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v  \aSEEK\vSEEK\v  \aLOC\vLOC\v  \aEOF\vEOF\v  \aTYPE\vTYPE\v
.context GET-graphics
.topic GET, PUT Statements (Graphics)
GET copies a rectangle of a graphics screen into an array.  PUT draws the
copy back anywhere on the screen.  For GET and PUT with files, see
\aGET, PUT (File I/O)\vGET\v.

\bGET\p [\bSTEP\p](x1!,y1!)-[\bSTEP\p](x2!,y2!), arrayname[(index%)]
\bPUT\p [\bSTEP\p](x1!,y1!), arrayname[(index%)] [,action]

    þ x1!,y1!,x2!,y2!   Opposite corners of the rectangle; for PUT,
                        the top left corner.  STEP makes a point
                        relative to the last point drawn.
    þ arrayname         A numeric array big enough for the picture.
    þ index%            The element the picture starts at; the first
                        element when left out.
    þ action            How PUT mixes the picture with the screen:
                          PSET    the picture as it was got
                          PRESET  the picture, colours inverted
                          AND     screen AND picture, bit by bit
                          OR      screen OR picture
                          XOR     screen XOR picture (the default)

The picture is kept as two INTEGERs, the width in bits and the height,
then the rows of pixels.  The bytes needed are

    4 + INT((width * bits + 7) / 8) * planes * height

with width and height in pixels, and bits and planes by screen mode:

    SCREEN 1         2 bits, 1 plane     SCREEN 7, 8, 9, 12   1 bit, 4 planes
    SCREEN 2, 11     1 bit, 1 plane      SCREEN 13            8 bits, 1 plane

SCREEN 10 here takes 2 bits in 1 plane; QBasic takes 1 bit in 2 planes.

A second PUT with XOR puts the screen back as it was, which is how a
shape is moved without spoiling what is behind it.  A rectangle not
wholly on the screen, or an array too small, gives "Illegal function
call", and so does either statement in SCREEN 0.  PUT leaves out the
pixels outside a VIEW.

Example:
    SCREEN 13
    CIRCLE (4, 4), 3, 14
    DIM ball%(33)                    ' 4 + 8 * 8 bytes, 2 to an element
    GET (0, 0)-(7, 7), ball%
    PUT (150, 90), ball%, PSET

See Also  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v  \aDIM\vDIM\v  \aBSAVE\vBSAVE\v  \aVIEW\vVIEW\v
.context GOSUB
.context RETURN
.topic GOSUB...RETURN Statements
GOSUB jumps to a subroutine: a group of lines in the same module or
procedure.  RETURN goes back to the statement after the GOSUB.

\bGOSUB\p {label | linenumber}
\bRETURN\p [{label | linenumber}]

    þ label, linenumber   Where the subroutine starts; for RETURN,
                          where to go instead of back.

GOSUBs may nest, and each RETURN goes back to the latest one.  A RETURN
with no GOSUB to go back to gives "RETURN without GOSUB".  RETURN with a
label forgets the way back and goes to the label.

A GOSUB in a SUB or FUNCTION reaches only labels in that procedure.
SUB and FUNCTION procedures, with variables and arguments of their own,
are usually the better choice.  The subroutines run by ON KEY, ON TIMER
and the other events also end with RETURN.

Example:
    INPUT "Radius"; radius
    GOSUB ShowArea
    END
    ShowArea:
        PRINT "Area:"; 3.14159 * radius ^ 2
        RETURN

See Also  \aON...GOSUB\vON-GOTO\v  \aGOTO\vGOTO\v  \aSUB\vSUB\v  \aFUNCTION\vFUNCTION\v  \aEvent Trapping\vevents\v
.context GOTO
.topic GOTO Statement
GOTO carries on with the program at a label or a line number, without
coming back.

\bGOTO\p {label | linenumber}

The label must be in the same module or procedure as the GOTO, so GOTO
cannot go into or out of a SUB or FUNCTION.  A label is a name and a
colon, first on its line; a line number is 0 to 65,529.

DO...LOOP, IF...THEN...ELSE and SELECT CASE cover most uses of GOTO, and
read more clearly.

Example:
    count = 0
    Again:
    count = count + 1
    IF count < 3 THEN GOTO Again
    PRINT count                      ' 3

See Also  \aGOSUB\vGOSUB\v  \aON...GOTO\vON-GOTO\v  \aIF\vIF\v  \aDO...LOOP\vDO\v
.context HEX$
.context OCT$
.topic HEX$, OCT$ Functions
HEX$ writes a whole number in hexadecimal digits, OCT$ in octal digits.

\bHEX$\p(numeric-expression&)
\bOCT$\p(numeric-expression&)

The argument is rounded to a whole number first; one outside the range
of a LONG gives "Overflow".  A negative number is written in two's
complement: 16 bits of it when the argument is an INTEGER, 32 bits
otherwise.  So HEX$(-1) is "FFFF" and HEX$(-1&) is "FFFFFFFF".  There is
no leading blank and no &H.

To read the digits back, put &H or &O in front and use VAL.

Example:
    PRINT HEX$(255), OCT$(8)         ' FF  10
    PRINT VAL("&H" + HEX$(4096))     ' 4096

See Also  \aVAL\vVAL\v  \aSTR$\vSTR$\v
.context IF
.context THEN
.context ELSE
.context ELSEIF
.topic IF...THEN...ELSE Statement
IF runs statements only when a condition is true, and others when the
condition is false.

On one line:

\bIF\p condition \bTHEN\p statements [\bELSE\p statements]
\bIF\p condition \bGOTO\p label [\bELSE\p statements]

As a block:

\bIF\p condition1 \bTHEN\p
    [statements]
[\bELSEIF\p condition2 \bTHEN\p
    [statements]]...
[\bELSE\p
    [statements]]
\bEND IF\p

    þ condition    A numeric expression; any value but 0 is true.  A
                   comparison gives -1 for true and 0 for false.
    þ statements   On one line, statements with colons between them.
                   A line number alone after THEN or ELSE is a GOTO.

In a block IF nothing follows THEN on its line but a comment.  The
statements under the first true condition run and the rest are skipped;
ELSE takes the case where none is true.  Every block needs its END IF.
A string as the condition gives "Type mismatch": compare strings with =,
<> and the rest instead.

Example:
    IF temp < 0 THEN
        PRINT "Freezing"
    ELSEIF temp < 20 THEN
        PRINT "Cool"
    END IF
    IF temp > 30 THEN PRINT "Hot" ELSE PRINT "Not hot"

See Also  \aSELECT CASE\vSELECT\v  \aON...GOTO\vON-GOTO\v  \aBoolean Operators\vboolean\v
.context INKEY$
.topic INKEY$ Function
INKEY$ reads one key from the keyboard, without waiting and without
showing the key.

\bINKEY$\p

The result is "" when no key is waiting, one character for an ordinary
key, and two characters - CHR$(0) and the scan code - for a key that has
no character: a function key, an arrow key, Home and the like.  Ctrl+Break
still stops the program.

Example:
    PRINT "Press Esc to stop."
    DO
        key$ = INKEY$
        IF LEN(key$) = 2 THEN PRINT "Scan code"; ASC(RIGHT$(key$, 1))
    LOOP UNTIL key$ = CHR$(27)

See Also  \aINPUT$\vINPUT$\v  \aINPUT\vINPUT\v  \aScan Codes\vscancodes\v  \aON KEY\vON-KEY\v  \aSLEEP\vSLEEP\v
.context INP
.topic INP Function
INP reads a byte from an I/O port.

\bINP\p(port&)

    þ port&   A port number, 0 to 65,535.

A port outside that range gives "Illegal function call".  The result is
0 to 255.

The read goes to the hardware, except for three ports that belong to
PM-DOS: 60h gives the scan code of the last key, 64h a keyboard status
whose bit 0 says a key is waiting, and 92h gives 2.  The VGA colour
ports 3C7h to 3C9h read the program's own palette.

Example:
    PRINT "Last scan code:"; INP(&H60)

See Also  \aOUT\vOUT\v  \aWAIT\vWAIT\v  \aPEEK\vPEEK\v
.context OUT
.topic OUT Statement
OUT writes a byte to an I/O port.

\bOUT\p port&, data%

    þ port&   A port number, 0 to 65,535.
    þ data%   The byte, 0 to 255.

Either one out of range gives "Illegal function call".  A byte sent to
the wrong port can hang the machine or upset a device.

OUT to 60h, 64h and 92h (the keyboard controller and the A20 gate) is
ignored: those belong to PM-DOS.  The VGA colour ports 3C7h, 3C8h and
3C9h set the program's own palette.

Example:
    SCREEN 13
    OUT &H3C8, 1                     ' colour 1 becomes
    OUT &H3C9, 63: OUT &H3C9, 0: OUT &H3C9, 0    ' red 63, green 0, blue 0
    LINE (0, 0)-(319, 199), 1, BF

See Also  \aINP\vINP\v  \aWAIT\vWAIT\v  \aPALETTE\vPALETTE\v  \aPOKE\vPOKE\v
.context INPUT
.topic INPUT Statement
INPUT reads values typed at the keyboard, or read from a file, into
variables.  For a whole line of text, see \aLINE INPUT\vLINE-INPUT\v.

\bINPUT\p [;] ["prompt"{; | ,}] variablelist
\bINPUT\p #filenumber%, variablelist

    þ ;              Straight after INPUT: the cursor stays on the line
                     when Enter is pressed.
    þ "prompt"       A string constant written first.  A ; after the
                     prompt adds a question mark, a , does not.  With
                     no prompt, "? " is written.
    þ variablelist   Variables with commas between them.  The values
                     typed have commas between them too.
    þ filenumber%    The number of a file open FOR INPUT.

The line typed is checked before anything is stored.  The wrong number
of values, or text where a number belongs, writes "Redo from start" and
asks again; a number too big for its variable writes "Overflow" first.
Enter alone gives every variable 0 or "".  A string typed in quotes may
hold commas.  Left, Right, Home, End, Ins, Del and Backspace edit the
line, Esc empties the line, and the line holds at most 255 characters.

From a file, values end at a comma or a line end, and a number also ends
at a blank.  Blanks before a value are skipped.  Reading past the end
gives "Input past end of file".  WRITE # writes values in the form that
INPUT # reads back best.

Example:
    INPUT "Name and age"; person$, age
    PRINT person$; " is"; age

See Also  \aLINE INPUT\vLINE-INPUT\v  \aINPUT$\vINPUT$\v  \aINKEY$\vINKEY$\v  \aWRITE\vWRITE\v  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v
.context LINE-INPUT
.topic LINE INPUT Statement
LINE INPUT reads a whole line - commas, quotes and all - into a string
variable.

\bLINE INPUT\p [;] ["prompt"{; | ,}] stringvariable$
\bLINE INPUT\p #filenumber%, stringvariable$

    þ ;                  Straight after LINE INPUT: the cursor stays on
                         the line when Enter is pressed.
    þ "prompt"           A string constant written first.  No question
                         mark is added.
    þ stringvariable$    Gets the line, without its carriage return.
    þ filenumber%        The number of a file open FOR INPUT.

From the keyboard the line holds at most 255 characters.  From a file a
line ends at a carriage return, and a line feed straight after that is
dropped; a line feed on its own stays in the text.  A Ctrl+Z ends the
file.  Reading past the end gives "Input past end of file".

Example:
    OPEN "README.TXT" FOR INPUT AS #1
    DO UNTIL EOF(1)
        LINE INPUT #1, text$
        PRINT text$
    LOOP
    CLOSE #1

See Also  \aINPUT\vINPUT\v  \aINPUT$\vINPUT$\v  \aEOF\vEOF\v  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v
.context INPUT$
.topic INPUT$ Function
INPUT$ reads a set number of characters from the keyboard or a file.

\bINPUT$\p(n& [, [#]filenumber%])

    þ n&            How many characters, 1 to 32,767.
    þ filenumber%   The number of an open file.  Left out, INPUT$
                    reads the keyboard.

From the keyboard INPUT$ waits for n& keys and does not show them.  Every
key counts, Enter too, which suits a one-key answer.  A key such as an
arrow gives CHR$(0), and its scan code as the next character.

From a file every byte counts, line ends and commas included; a RANDOM
or BINARY file is read from its current position.

n& out of range gives "Illegal function call", and reading past the end
of a file gives "Input past end of file".

Example:
    PRINT "Continue (Y/N)?"
    answer$ = UCASE$(INPUT$(1))
    OPEN "PICTURE.BIN" FOR BINARY AS #1
    header$ = INPUT$(4, #1)

See Also  \aINKEY$\vINKEY$\v  \aINPUT\vINPUT\v  \aLINE INPUT\vLINE-INPUT\v  \aGET, PUT\vGET\v
.context INSTR
.topic INSTR Function
INSTR finds where one string first appears inside another.

\bINSTR\p([start%,] string$, find$)

    þ start%    Where to start looking; 1 when left out.
    þ string$   The string to search.
    þ find$     The string to look for.

The result is the position of the first character of the match, or 0
when there is none.  Capitals and small letters differ.  An empty find$
gives start% (0 when start% is past the end of string$).  A start% below
1 or above 32,767 gives "Illegal function call".

Example:
    path$ = "C:\\GAMES\\SNAKE.BAS"
    slash% = INSTR(path$, "\\")
    PRINT INSTR(slash% + 1, path$, "\\")      ' 9

See Also  \aMID$\vMID$\v  \aLEFT$\vLEFT$\v  \aLEN\vLEN\v  \aUCASE$\vUCASE$\v
.context INT
.topic INT Function
INT rounds a number down: the result is the largest whole number that is
not more than the argument.

\bINT\p(numeric-expression)

INT(2.8) is 2 and INT(-2.2) is -3.  FIX cuts toward zero instead, and
CINT rounds to the nearest.  The result has the type of the argument.

INT(x + .5) rounds x to the nearest whole number, and INT(x * 100 + .5)
/ 100 to two decimal places.

Example:
    PRINT INT(9.99), INT(-0.5)       ' 9  -1
    PRINT INT(3.14159 * 100 + .5) / 100      ' 3.14

See Also  \aFIX\vFIX\v  \aCINT\vCINT\v  \aCLNG\vCLNG\v
.context IOCTL
.context IOCTL$
.topic IOCTL Statement, IOCTL$ Function
IOCTL sends a control string to a device driver that is open as a file;
IOCTL$ reads one back from the driver.

\bIOCTL\p [#]filenumber%, string$
\bIOCTL$\p([#]filenumber%)

    þ filenumber%   The number of a device opened with OPEN.
    þ string$       The control string.  What it means is up to the
                    driver.

Only drivers made to take control strings answer.  In this QBasic none
do: IOCTL and IOCTL$ check the file number, then give "Illegal function
call".

See Also  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v
.context KEY
.context LIST
.topic KEY Statement
KEY gives the function keys text, and shows, hides or lists that text on
the screen.  For trapping keys, see \aKEY(n), ON KEY\vON-KEY\v.

\bKEY\p n%, string$
\bKEY ON\p
\bKEY OFF\p
\bKEY LIST\p

    þ n%        1 to 10 for F1 to F10, 30 and 31 for F11 and F12; or
                15 to 25 for a key to trap (see below).
    þ string$   For a function key, up to 15 characters.

KEY ON shows the first six characters of each of F1 to F10 on the bottom
row, and LOCATE cannot reach that row while they are shown.  KEY OFF
takes the row away.  KEY LIST writes the text of F1 to F12 down the
screen.  A longer string$ is cut to 15 characters; an n% out of range
gives "Illegal function call".

For n% 15 to 25, string$ is CHR$(flags) + CHR$(scancode): the shift keys
that must be down, and the scan code of the key.  The flags add up from
1, 2 or 3 for Shift, 4 for Ctrl, 8 for Alt, 32 for Num Lock, 64 for Caps
Lock and 128 for a grey key of a 101-key keyboard.  KEY(n) and ON KEY(n)
then trap that key.  With Caps Lock or Num Lock on, the key is trapped
only if the flags include 64 or 32.

In QBasic a function key given text types the text when pressed.  This
QBasic shows and lists the text but does not type the text.

Example:
    KEY 1, "Help"
    KEY 2, "Save"
    KEY ON
    KEY 15, CHR$(4) + CHR$(&H1F)     ' Ctrl+S, for KEY(15)

See Also  \aKEY(n), ON KEY\vON-KEY\v  \aINKEY$\vINKEY$\v  \aScan Codes\vscancodes\v
.context ON-KEY
.topic KEY(n), ON KEY Statements
ON KEY(n) names a subroutine to run when a key is pressed.  KEY(n) turns
the trapping of that key on or off, or holds it.

\bON KEY\p(n%) \bGOSUB\p {label | linenumber}
\bKEY\p(n%) {\bON\p | \bOFF\p | \bSTOP\p}

    þ n%      The key:
                1 to 10    F1 to F10
                11 to 14   Up, Left, Right, Down
                15 to 25   keys defined with KEY n%, string$
                30, 31     F11, F12
                0          every one of the above (KEY(0) only)
    þ label   The first line of the subroutine, in the module's code.

Nothing happens until KEY(n) ON.  The subroutine ends with RETURN, which
goes back to where the program was.  ON KEY(n) GOSUB 0 ends the trapping.
KEY(n) OFF stops the trapping; KEY(n) STOP remembers a press and runs the
subroutine at the next KEY(n) ON.  See \aEvent Trapping\vevents\v.

Example:
    ON KEY(10) GOSUB Finish
    KEY(10) ON
    PRINT "Counting.  F10 stops."
    DO: total& = total& + 1: LOOP
    Finish: PRINT total&: END

See Also  \aKEY\vKEY\v  \aEvent Trapping\vevents\v  \aRETURN\vRETURN\v
.context KILL
.topic KILL Statement
KILL deletes files from a disk.

\bKILL\p filespec$

    þ filespec$   The file to delete, with drive and path if needed.
                  * and ? in the name delete every file that matches.

Directories are left alone; RMDIR removes those.  No file matching gives
"File not found", and a file the program has open gives "File already
open".  There is no undo.

Example:
    KILL "*.BAK"
    KILL "C:\\TEMP\\OLD.TXT"

See Also  \aNAME\vNAME\v  \aFILES\vFILES\v  \aRMDIR\vRMDIR\v
.context LBOUND
.context UBOUND
.topic LBOUND, UBOUND Functions
LBOUND gives the lowest subscript a dimension of an array allows, UBOUND
the highest.

\bLBOUND\p(array[, dimension%])
\bUBOUND\p(array[, dimension%])

    þ array        The array's name, without parentheses.
    þ dimension%   Which dimension: 1 for the first, and so on; 1 when
                   left out.

A dimension the array does not have, or an array not dimensioned (a
dynamic array after ERASE, say), gives "Subscript out of range".  They
are how a SUB or FUNCTION finds the size of an array passed to it.

Example:
    DIM board(1 TO 8, 0 TO 7)
    PRINT LBOUND(board), UBOUND(board)       ' 1  8
    PRINT LBOUND(board, 2), UBOUND(board, 2) ' 0  7

See Also  \aDIM\vDIM\v  \aOPTION BASE\vOPTION\v  \aERASE\vERASE\v
.context LCASE$
.context UCASE$
.topic LCASE$, UCASE$ Functions
LCASE$ gives a copy of a string in small letters, UCASE$ in capitals.

\bLCASE$\p(stringexpression$)
\bUCASE$\p(stringexpression$)

Only the letters A to Z change; digits, punctuation and accented letters
stay as they are.  Comparing the UCASE$ of two strings ignores case.

Example:
    INPUT "Again (yes/no)"; reply$
    IF UCASE$(LEFT$(reply$, 1)) = "Y" THEN RUN

See Also  \aLEFT$\vLEFT$\v  \aINSTR\vINSTR\v
.context LEFT$
.context RIGHT$
.topic LEFT$, RIGHT$ Functions
LEFT$ gives the first characters of a string, RIGHT$ the last ones.

\bLEFT$\p(stringexpression$, n%)
\bRIGHT$\p(stringexpression$, n%)

    þ n%   How many characters, 0 to 32,767.  0 gives "", and more than
           the string holds gives the whole string.

A negative n% gives "Illegal function call".

Example:
    file$ = "REPORT.TXT"
    PRINT LEFT$(file$, 6), RIGHT$(file$, 3)  ' REPORT  TXT

See Also  \aMID$\vMID$\v  \aLEN\vLEN\v  \aINSTR\vINSTR\v  \aLTRIM$\vLTRIM$\v
.context LEN
.topic LEN Function
LEN gives the number of characters in a string, or the number of bytes a
variable takes.

\bLEN\p(stringexpression$)
\bLEN\p(variable)

    þ stringexpression$   Any string: LEN gives its length.
    þ variable            A variable of another type: 2 for INTEGER, 4
                          for LONG and SINGLE, 8 for DOUBLE, and the
                          size of a record or a fixed-length string.

LEN(variable) is the usual way to give OPEN the record length of a record
variable.

Example:
    PRINT LEN("QBasic")              ' 6
    DIM stock AS STRING * 12
    PRINT LEN(stock)                 ' 12

See Also  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v  \aTYPE\vTYPE\v  \aData Types\vtypes\v
.context LET
.topic LET Statement
LET gives a variable a value.  The word LET may be left out.

[\bLET\p] variable = expression

    þ variable     A variable, an array element or a record's element.
    þ expression   A value that suits the variable: a number for a
                   numeric variable, a string for a string one.

A number is converted to the variable's type, and rounded when it goes
into an INTEGER or LONG; one too big for the type gives "Overflow".  A
string into a numeric variable, or a number into a string variable, gives
"Type mismatch".  A whole record can be assigned from another variable of
the same TYPE.

Example:
    LET total = 0
    total = total + 2.5
    count% = 7.6                     ' 8

See Also  \aLSET\vLSET\v  \aMID$\vMID$\v  \aSWAP\vSWAP\v  \aData Types\vtypes\v
.context LINE
.topic LINE Statement
LINE draws a straight line, or a box, on a graphics screen.  LINE INPUT
is another statement: see \aLINE INPUT\vLINE-INPUT\v.

\bLINE\p [[\bSTEP\p](x1!,y1!)]-[\bSTEP\p](x2!,y2!) [,[color%] [,[\bB\p | \bBF\p] [,style%]]]

    þ STEP         Makes a point relative: the first to the last point
                   drawn, the second to the first.
    þ x1!,y1!      Where the line starts; left out, at the last point
                   drawn.
    þ x2!,y2!      Where the line ends.
    þ color%       The colour; the foreground colour when left out.
    þ B            Draws a box with the two points as opposite corners.
    þ BF           Draws the box filled.
    þ style%       A 16-bit pattern for the line, top bit first and
                   over again: a 1 bit draws a pixel, a 0 bit leaves
                   the pixel as it was.  BF draws no pattern.

The end point becomes the last point drawn, for STEP and for the next
LINE that leaves out its first point.  WINDOW and VIEW, when set, change
the coordinates, and a line is cut off at the edge of the VIEW.  LINE in
SCREEN 0 gives "Illegal function call".

Example:
    SCREEN 12
    LINE (0, 0)-(639, 479), 14
    LINE (100, 100)-STEP(200, 150), 4, BF
    LINE -(100, 100), 15
    LINE (50, 400)-(590, 400), 7, , &HF0F0

See Also  \aPSET\vPSET\v  \aCIRCLE\vCIRCLE\v  \aDRAW\vDRAW\v  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v  \aWINDOW\vWINDOW\v  \aVIEW\vVIEW\v
.context LOC
.topic LOC Function
LOC tells where in an open file the last read or write was.

\bLOC\p(filenumber%)

    þ filenumber%   The number of an open file.

What the number means depends on how the file was opened:

    RANDOM       the number of the last record read or written
    BINARY       the position of the last byte read or written
    sequential   the position in the file, in blocks of 128 bytes

Example:
    OPEN "SCORES.DAT" FOR RANDOM AS #1 LEN = 4
    GET #1, 10
    PRINT LOC(1)                     ' 10

See Also  \aSEEK\vSEEK\v  \aLOF\vLOF\v  \aEOF\vEOF\v  \aGET, PUT\vGET\v
.context LOCATE
.topic LOCATE Statement
LOCATE moves the text cursor, and sets whether the cursor shows and what
shape it has.

\bLOCATE\p [row%] [,[column%] [,[cursor%] [,[start%] [,stop%]]]]

    þ row%, column%   Where the next PRINT goes.  Row 1 is the top
                      line and column 1 the left edge.
    þ cursor%         1 shows the cursor while the program runs, 0
                      hides the cursor.
    þ start%, stop%   The first and last scan lines of the cursor, 0
                      to 31; stop% is start% when left out.

Only what is given changes.  The row must be on the screen, inside the
VIEW PRINT area when one is set, and not the bottom row while KEY ON
shows the function keys there.  The column must be inside the width of
the screen.  A value out of range gives "Illegal function call".

Example:
    CLS
    LOCATE 12, 36: PRINT "Centre"
    LOCATE 24, 1, 1                  ' row 24, cursor showing

See Also  \aCSRLIN\vCSRLIN\v  \aPOS\vPOS\v  \aVIEW PRINT\vVIEW-PRINT\v  \aWIDTH\vWIDTH\v  \aKEY\vKEY\v
.context LOCK
.context UNLOCK
.topic LOCK, UNLOCK Statements
LOCK keeps other programs on a network from using all or part of an open
file; UNLOCK lets them use it again.

\bLOCK\p [#]filenumber% [, {record& | [start&] \bTO\p end&}]
\bUNLOCK\p [#]filenumber% [, {record& | [start&] \bTO\p end&}]

    þ filenumber%    The number of an open file.
    þ record&        One record (RANDOM) or byte (BINARY).
    þ start&, end&   The first and last record or byte; start& is 1
                     when left out.

With nothing after the file number the whole file is locked.  An UNLOCK
must name the same records as a LOCK before it, and every lock should be
undone before the file is closed.  A sequential file is always locked
whole.

Under MS-DOS, LOCK needs SHARE.EXE.  In this QBasic, LOCK and UNLOCK
check the file number and do nothing else.

Example:
    OPEN "STOCK.DAT" FOR RANDOM SHARED AS #1 LEN = 40
    LOCK #1, 5
    GET #1, 5
    PUT #1, 5
    UNLOCK #1, 5

See Also  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v
.context LOF
.topic LOF Function
LOF gives the length of an open file in bytes.

\bLOF\p(filenumber%)

    þ filenumber%   The number of an open file.

For a RANDOM file, LOF divided by the record length is the number of
records.  A device (SCRN:, LPT1: and the rest) gives 0 here.

Example:
    OPEN "PEOPLE.DAT" FOR RANDOM AS #1 LEN = 32
    PRINT "Records:"; LOF(1) \\ 32

See Also  \aLOC\vLOC\v  \aEOF\vEOF\v  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v
.context LOG
.topic LOG Function
LOG gives the natural logarithm of a number: the power that e
(2.718282...) must be raised to, to make the number.

\bLOG\p(numeric-expression)

The argument must be above 0, or the error is "Illegal function call".
The result is SINGLE unless the argument is DOUBLE.  For a logarithm to
base 10, divide by LOG(10).

Example:
    INPUT "A number above 0"; value
    PRINT "Natural log:"; LOG(value)
    PRINT "Log to base 10:"; LOG(value) / LOG(10)

See Also  \aEXP\vEXP\v
.context LPOS
.topic LPOS Function
LPOS gives the column the printer prints at next, as LPRINT has counted
it.

\bLPOS\p(n%)

    þ n%   The printer: 0 or 1 for LPT1, 2 for LPT2, 3 for LPT3.

The count starts at 1 and goes back to 1 after each carriage return or
line feed sent.  This QBasic has one printer, PRN, and gives its column
whatever n% is.

Example:
    FOR i = 1 TO 50
        LPRINT i;
        IF LPOS(1) > 70 THEN LPRINT
    NEXT i

See Also  \aLPRINT\vLPRINT\v  \aPOS\vPOS\v  \aWIDTH\vWIDTH\v
.context LSET
.context RSET
.topic LSET, RSET Statements
LSET puts a string into a string variable against its left end, RSET
against its right end, without changing the variable's length.

\bLSET\p stringvariable$ = stringexpression$
\bRSET\p stringvariable$ = stringexpression$
\bLSET\p recordvariable1 = recordvariable2

A shorter string is filled out with blanks (on the right by LSET, on the
left by RSET); a longer one is cut off on the right.  An empty variable
stays empty.  This is how text goes into the variables FIELD names.

LSET also copies one record variable into another of a different TYPE,
byte for byte: what does not fit is dropped, and any room left over is
filled with blanks.

Example:
    title$ = SPACE$(10)
    RSET title$ = "Total"
    PRINT "["; title$; "]"           ' [     Total]

See Also  \aFIELD\vFIELD\v  \aMID$\vMID$\v  \aLET\vLET\v  \aSPACE$\vSPACE$\v
.context LTRIM$
.context RTRIM$
.topic LTRIM$, RTRIM$ Functions
LTRIM$ takes the blanks off the start of a string, RTRIM$ off the end.

\bLTRIM$\p(stringexpression$)
\bRTRIM$\p(stringexpression$)

Only blanks, CHR$(32), are taken; tabs stay.  LTRIM$ suits STR$ of a
number, which starts with a blank; RTRIM$ suits fixed-length strings and
FIELD variables, which are often filled out with blanks.

Example:
    PRINT "["; LTRIM$(STR$(42)); "]"         ' [42]
    PRINT "["; RTRIM$("Done   "); "]"        ' [Done]

See Also  \aSTR$\vSTR$\v  \aLEFT$\vLEFT$\v  \aMID$\vMID$\v
.context MID$
.topic MID$ Function and Statement
MID$ as a function gives part of a string.  MID$ as a statement puts
characters into a string variable in place of the ones there.

\bMID$\p(stringexpression$, start% [, length%])
\bMID$\p(stringvariable$, start% [, length%]) = stringexpression$

    þ start%    The first character to take or replace; 1 is the
                first character of the string.
    þ length%   How many characters.  Left out: all the rest (the
                function), or all of stringexpression$ (the
                statement).

The function gives "" when start% is past the end, and stops at the end
of the string when length% runs beyond.  A start% below 1, or a negative
length%, gives "Illegal function call".

The statement never changes the variable's length: what does not fit is
dropped.  start% must be inside the variable's text.

Example:
    word$ = "basic"
    PRINT MID$(word$, 2, 3)          ' asi
    MID$(word$, 1, 1) = "B"
    PRINT word$                      ' Basic

See Also  \aLEFT$\vLEFT$\v  \aRIGHT$\vRIGHT$\v  \aINSTR\vINSTR\v  \aLEN\vLEN\v
.context MKI$
.context MKL$
.context MKS$
.context MKD$
.topic MKI$, MKL$, MKS$, MKD$ Functions
These make a string of the bytes that hold a number, so that the number
can go into a FIELD variable of a random-access file.  CVI, CVL, CVS and
CVD turn the string back into the number.

\bMKI$\p(integer-expression%)
\bMKL$\p(long-expression&)
\bMKS$\p(single-expression!)
\bMKD$\p(double-expression#)

    MKI$  2 bytes      MKL$  4 bytes      MKS$  4 bytes      MKD$  8 bytes

The bytes are the number's own, lowest first; MKS$ and MKD$ are IEEE
floating point.  With QBASIC /MBF, MKS$ and MKD$ make Microsoft Binary
Format, as MKSMBF$ and MKDMBF$ do.  STR$ is different: it writes the
number in digits.

Example:
    OPEN "PRICES.DAT" FOR RANDOM AS #1 LEN = 10
    FIELD #1, 2 AS qty$, 8 AS price$
    LSET qty$ = MKI$(12)
    LSET price$ = MKD$(4.99#)
    PUT #1, 1

See Also  \aCVI\vCVI\v  \aMKSMBF$\vMKSMBF$\v  \aFIELD\vFIELD\v  \aSTR$\vSTR$\v
.context MKSMBF$
.context MKDMBF$
.topic MKSMBF$, MKDMBF$ Functions
MKSMBF$ and MKDMBF$ make the 4 or 8 bytes of a number in Microsoft Binary
Format, the floating point of GW-BASIC and of QuickBASIC before 4.0, for
files that those programs read.

\bMKSMBF$\p(single-expression!)
\bMKDMBF$\p(double-expression#)

A number too big for the format gives "Overflow"; one too small for it
becomes 0.  CVSMBF and CVDMBF read the bytes back.  QBASIC /MBF makes MKS$
and MKD$ work the same way.

Example:
    OPEN "OLDDATA.DAT" FOR RANDOM AS #1 LEN = 4
    FIELD #1, 4 AS amount$
    LSET amount$ = MKSMBF$(19.95)
    PUT #1, 1

See Also  \aCVSMBF\vCVSMBF\v  \aMKS$\vMKS$\v  \aFIELD\vFIELD\v
.context NAME
.topic NAME Statement
NAME gives a file a new name, or moves the file to another directory on
the same drive.

\bNAME\p oldspec$ \bAS\p newspec$

    þ oldspec$   The file as it is now.
    þ newspec$   The new name, with a path to move the file.

A newspec$ that already exists gives "File already exists", an oldspec$
that does not exist gives "File not found", an oldspec$ that is open gives
"File already open", and a newspec$ on another drive gives "Rename
across disks".

Example:
    NAME "REPORT.TXT" AS "REPORT.OLD"
    NAME "C:\\TEMP\\LIST.TXT" AS "C:\\DOCS\\LIST.TXT"

See Also  \aKILL\vKILL\v  \aFILES\vFILES\v
.context ON
.topic ON Keyword
ON starts several statements, and in others turns something on.

Statements that start with ON:

    þ \aON ERROR GOTO\vON-ERROR\v           where to go when an error happens
    þ \aON n GOTO, ON n GOSUB\vON-GOTO\v   one of a list of labels, by number
    þ \aON KEY(n) GOSUB\vON-KEY\v         a subroutine for a key
    þ \aON TIMER(n) GOSUB\vTIMER\v       a subroutine every n seconds
    þ \aON PLAY(n) GOSUB\vevents\v        a subroutine when the music runs low
    þ \aON COM(n) GOSUB\vCOM\v         a subroutine for a serial port
    þ \aON PEN GOSUB\vPEN\v            a subroutine for the light pen
    þ \aON STRIG(n) GOSUB\vSTRIG\v       a subroutine for a joystick button

ON after an event - KEY(n) ON, TIMER ON, PLAY ON and the rest - starts
the trapping: see \aEvent Trapping\vevents\v.  KEY ON shows the function
keys' text: see \aKEY\vKEY\v.
.context ON-ERROR
.topic ON ERROR Statement
ON ERROR GOTO names an error handler: lines the program goes to when a
run-time error happens, instead of stopping.

\bON ERROR GOTO\p {label | linenumber}
\bON ERROR GOTO 0\p

    þ label   The handler's first line, in the module's code (not in a
              SUB or FUNCTION).
    þ 0       Turns error handling off: the next error stops the
              program with its message.

In the handler, ERR gives the error's code and ERL its line number.  The
handler ends with RESUME, which goes back to the statement that failed;
RESUME NEXT, which goes on after that statement; or RESUME label.  An
error in a SUB or FUNCTION goes to the module's handler too, and RESUME
and RESUME NEXT go back into the procedure.

ON ERROR GOTO 0 in the handler stops the program with the error being
handled, and so does a second error in the handler.  A handler that runs
on to the end of the program stops it with "No RESUME"; an END in the
handler ends the program quietly.

Example:
    ON ERROR GOTO NoFile
    OPEN "SETTINGS.INI" FOR INPUT AS #1
    END
    NoFile:
    PRINT "No settings: error"; ERR
    RESUME NEXT

See Also  \aRESUME\vRESUME\v  \aERR\vERR\v  \aERROR\vERROR\v  \aError Codes\verrcodes\v
.context ON-GOTO
.topic ON...GOSUB, ON...GOTO Statements
ON...GOTO jumps to one of a list of labels, picked by a number.
ON...GOSUB calls one of them as a subroutine.

\bON\p expression \bGOTO\p labellist
\bON\p expression \bGOSUB\p labellist

    þ expression   Rounded to a whole number: 1 picks the first label,
                   2 the second, and so on.
    þ labellist    Labels or line numbers, with commas between them.

A value of 0, or one beyond the end of the list, goes on to the next
statement.  A negative value, or one above 255, gives "Illegal function
call".  After ON...GOSUB, RETURN comes back to the statement after the
ON.  SELECT CASE does the same work and is easier to read.

Example:
    FOR pick% = 0 TO 2
        ON pick% GOSUB First, Second
    NEXT pick%
    END
    First: PRINT "one": RETURN
    Second: PRINT "two": RETURN

See Also  \aGOSUB\vGOSUB\v  \aGOTO\vGOTO\v  \aSELECT CASE\vSELECT\v
.context events
.context OFF
.topic Event Trapping
A program can have a subroutine run when something happens - a key
pressed, time passing, the music running low - without looking for the
event itself.  ON event GOSUB names the subroutine, and event ON starts
the trapping.

    Event      Named with                Happens when
    KEY(n)     ON KEY(n) GOSUB label     key n is pressed
    TIMER      ON TIMER(n) GOSUB label   every n seconds (1 to 86,400)
    PLAY       ON PLAY(n) GOSUB label    fewer than n notes are left to
                                         play in the background (1 to 32)
    COM(n)     ON COM(n) GOSUB label     serial port n receives data
    PEN        ON PEN GOSUB label        the light pen is pressed
    STRIG(n)   ON STRIG(n) GOSUB label   a joystick button is pressed

Then, for each event (KEY(1) ON, TIMER OFF, PLAY STOP and so on):

    þ event ON     Trapping on: the subroutine runs as soon as the
                   event happens, between one statement and the next.
    þ event OFF    Trapping off: the event is ignored and forgotten.
    þ event STOP   The event is remembered but not acted on; the
                   subroutine runs when event ON comes again.

The subroutine ends with RETURN, which goes back to the statement the
program was at.  While the subroutine runs, its own event is held as if
stopped, and dealt with after the RETURN.  ON event GOSUB 0 ends the
trapping.  The labels must be in the module's code.

In this QBasic COM, PEN and STRIG events never happen; the statements are
taken and do nothing.

Example:
    ON TIMER(1) GOSUB Tick
    TIMER ON
    DO: LOOP UNTIL INKEY$ <> ""
    END
    Tick: LOCATE 1, 70: PRINT TIME$: RETURN

See Also  \aON KEY\vON-KEY\v  \aTIMER\vTIMER\v  \aPLAY\vPLAY\v  \aCOM\vCOM\v  \aPEN\vPEN\v  \aSTRIG\vSTRIG\v  \aRETURN\vRETURN\v
.context OPEN
.context ACCESS
.context APPEND
.context BINARY
.context OUTPUT
.context RANDOM
.topic OPEN Statement
OPEN opens a file or a device under a number, which the other file
statements then use.

\bOPEN\p file$ [\bFOR\p mode] [\bACCESS\p access] [lock] \bAS\p [#]filenumber% [\bLEN\p=reclen%]
\bOPEN\p mode$, [#]filenumber%, file$ [, reclen%]

    þ file$         The file, with drive and path if needed, or a
                    device: SCRN:, KYBD:, CONS:, LPT1: to LPT3:, COM1:
                    to COM4:.
    þ mode          INPUT    read text from the start
                    OUTPUT   write text to a new, empty file
                    APPEND   write text on the end of the file
                    RANDOM   read and write records (the default)
                    BINARY   read and write bytes anywhere
    þ access        READ, WRITE or READ WRITE: what this program will
                    do with the file.
    þ lock          SHARED, LOCK READ, LOCK WRITE or LOCK READ WRITE:
                    what other programs may do with the file meanwhile.
    þ filenumber%   1 to 255, and not in use already (see FREEFILE).
    þ reclen%       For RANDOM, the record length: 1 to 32,767 bytes,
                    128 when left out.
    þ mode$         In the second form: "I", "O", "A", "R" or "B".

OUTPUT empties a file that is there; APPEND, RANDOM and BINARY make a
file that is not, and INPUT of a missing file gives "File not found".  A
file that is open already may be opened again, under another number, FOR
INPUT, RANDOM or BINARY; FOR OUTPUT or APPEND it gives "File already
open".

Other errors: a number in use, "File already open"; a number out of
range, "Bad file name or number"; * or ? in the name, "Bad file name"; a
bad LEN, "Bad record length"; KYBD: for output or SCRN: for input, "Bad
file mode"; a printer or port that is not there, "Device unavailable".

In this QBasic the access and lock are taken and ignored, LEN does not
set the buffer size of a sequential file, and the settings after COMn:
(speed, parity and the rest) are not applied: the DOS device is opened
as it stands.

Example:
    OPEN "SCORES.TXT" FOR OUTPUT AS #1
    WRITE #1, "Ann", 120
    CLOSE #1
    OPEN "SCORES.TXT" FOR APPEND AS #1
    WRITE #1, "Bob", 95
    CLOSE #1

See Also  \aCLOSE\vCLOSE\v  \aFREEFILE\vFREEFILE\v  \aINPUT\vINPUT\v  \aPRINT\vPRINT\v  \aGET, PUT\vGET\v  \aFIELD\vFIELD\v  \aAS\vAS\v
.context AS
.topic AS Keyword
AS comes before what a statement is being given: a type, a file number,
a variable or a new name.

    þ A variable's type:          \aDIM\vDIM\v  \aREDIM\vREDIM\v  \aCOMMON\vCOMMON\v  \aSHARED\vSHARED\v  \aSTATIC\vSTATIC\v
    þ A parameter's type:         \aSUB\vSUB\v  \aFUNCTION\vFUNCTION\v  \aDECLARE\vDECLARE\v  \aDEF FN\vDEF\v
    þ A record element's type:    \aTYPE\vTYPE\v
    þ The number of a file:       \aOPEN\vOPEN\v
    þ A variable for part of a
        random-access record:     \aFIELD\vFIELD\v
    þ A file's new name:          \aNAME\vNAME\v

The types are INTEGER, LONG, SINGLE, DOUBLE, STRING, STRING * n for a
fixed length, and the name of a TYPE; DECLARE also takes ANY.  See
\aData Types\vtypes\v.
.context OPTION
.context BASE
.topic OPTION BASE Statement
OPTION BASE sets the lowest subscript of the arrays that do not give
their own.

\bOPTION BASE\p {0 | 1}

Without OPTION BASE the lowest subscript is 0.  The statement goes in
the module's code before any array is dimensioned, and only once: a
second one gives "Duplicate definition".  An array whose bounds are given
as lower TO upper is not affected.

Example:
    OPTION BASE 1
    DIM month$(12)                   ' month$(1) to month$(12)
    PRINT LBOUND(month$)             ' 1

See Also  \aDIM\vDIM\v  \aLBOUND\vLBOUND\v  \aREDIM\vREDIM\v
.context PAINT
.topic PAINT Statement
PAINT fills an area of a graphics screen with a colour or a pattern.  The
fill starts at a point and spreads out until it meets a border colour.

\bPAINT\p [STEP] (x!, y!) [, [paint] [, [bordercolour%] [, background$]]]

    þ STEP             x! and y! are offsets from the last point drawn.
    þ x!, y!           Where the fill starts.
    þ paint            A colour number, or a tile string (see below).
                       Without one, the foreground colour.
    þ bordercolour%    The colour that stops the fill.  Without one,
                       the paint colour.
    þ background$      See below.

The fill spreads up, down, left and right, as far as the border colour or
the edge of the viewport.  The border must be closed: one gap lets the
paint out over the rest of the screen.  Nothing is painted when the start
point is outside the viewport or already in the border colour.

A tile string paints a pattern that repeats across and down the area.
Each byte is one row of the pattern, except in the 16-colour modes:
    SCREEN 1          a byte is 4 pixels, 2 bits each
    SCREEN 2, 11      a byte is 8 pixels, 1 bit each
    SCREEN 7-9, 12    4 bytes are a row of 8 pixels: one byte for each
                      bit of the colour number, bit 0 first
    SCREEN 10         2 bytes are a row of 8 pixels, the same way
    SCREEN 13         a byte is 1 pixel: its colour
The tile may be up to 64 bytes long.  An empty tile is "Illegal function
call", and so is PAINT on a text screen.

In QBasic, background$ names a tile row already on the screen that the
fill may paint over.  This QBasic accepts background$ and does not need it.

Example:
    SCREEN 12
    CIRCLE (320, 240), 100, 14
    PAINT (320, 240), 1, 14                ' blue inside the yellow ring
    tile$ = CHR$(&HAA) + CHR$(0) + CHR$(0) + CHR$(0)
    PAINT (5, 5), tile$, 14                ' blue stripes outside it

See Also  \aCIRCLE\vCIRCLE\v  \aLINE\vLINE\v  \aDRAW\vDRAW\v  \aCOLOR\vCOLOR\v  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v
.context PALETTE
.topic PALETTE, PALETTE USING Statements
PALETTE changes the colour an attribute (a colour number the program draws
with) shows on the screen.  PALETTE USING changes all of them from an
array.

\bPALETTE\p [attribute%, colour&]
\bPALETTE USING\p arrayname[(index%)]

    þ attribute%       The colour number to change.
    þ colour&          The colour it is to show (see below).  -1 leaves
                       the attribute as it is.
    þ arrayname        An INTEGER or LONG array with a colour for each
                       attribute, attribute 0 first.
    þ index%           The element that holds attribute 0's colour.
                       Without one, the array's first element.

PALETTE with nothing after it puts back the mode's own colours.

What a colour number means depends on the screen mode:
    SCREEN 0            0-63, the EGA colours
    SCREEN 1, 2, 7, 8   0-15, the sixteen standard colours
    SCREEN 9            0-63, the EGA colours
    SCREEN 10           0-8
    SCREEN 11, 12, 13   65536 * blue + 256 * green + red, each 0 to 63

An attribute the mode does not have, or a colour out of range, is
"Illegal function call".  So is an array too short for every attribute.

In this QBasic, PALETTE in SCREEN 0 checks its numbers and changes nothing,
and SCREEN 10's nine colours are steady greys (QBasic makes some blink).

Example:
    SCREEN 13
    FOR i% = 0 TO 63
        PALETTE i%, i%                    ' attributes 0-63: reds
    NEXT i%
    DIM pal&(255): PALETTE USING pal&(0)  ' every attribute black

See Also  \aCOLOR\vCOLOR\v  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v  \aScreen Modes\vscreenmodes\v
.context PCOPY
.topic PCOPY Statement
PCOPY copies one screen page over another.

\bPCOPY\p sourcepage%, destinationpage%

    þ sourcepage%       The page to copy.
    þ destinationpage%  The page that is overwritten.

The pages a mode has are listed under \aScreen Modes\vscreenmodes\v.  A
page the mode does not have is "Illegal function call".

PCOPY goes with SCREEN's active and visible pages: draw the next picture
on a hidden page, then copy it to the page on show, and the picture
changes at once instead of being seen half drawn.

Example:
    SCREEN 7, , 1, 0                  ' draw on page 1, show page 0
    LINE (0, 0)-(319, 199), 4, BF
    PCOPY 1, 0                        ' the red screen appears at once

See Also  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v  \aCLS\vCLS\v
.context PEEK
.topic PEEK Function
PEEK returns the byte at an address in the segment set by DEF SEG.

\bPEEK\p(address)

    þ address          The offset in the segment, 0 to 65535.

The result is 0 to 255.  A negative address counts back from 65536; one
outside -32768 to 65535 is "Overflow".

This QBasic has no real-mode memory: PEEK reaches the program's own
variables, the screen at &HB800 and &HA000, the BIOS data area at &H40 and
the ROMs.  Any other address reads 0.  See \aQBasic on PM-DOS\vpmdos\v.

Example:
    DEF SEG = &H40
    IF PEEK(&H17) AND 64 THEN PRINT "Caps Lock is on"
    DEF SEG

See Also  \aPOKE\vPOKE\v  \aDEF SEG\vSEG\v  \aVARPTR\vVARPTR\v  \aVARSEG\vVARSEG\v  \aSADD\vSADD\v
.context POKE
.topic POKE Statement
POKE writes a byte at an address in the segment set by DEF SEG.

\bPOKE\p address, byte%

    þ address          The offset in the segment, 0 to 65535.
    þ byte%            The value to write.  Only its low 8 bits are
                       stored.

A POKE in the wrong place can change the program's variables without
warning.  An address or value outside -32768 to 65535 is "Overflow".

This QBasic has no real-mode memory: POKE reaches the program's own
variables and the screen at &HB800 (text) and &HA000 (SCREEN 13).  In the
BIOS data area only the keyboard buffer takes a POKE; anything else is
ignored.  See \aQBasic on PM-DOS\vpmdos\v.

Example:
    DEF SEG = &HB800
    POKE 0, ASC("A")                  ' an A in the top left corner,
    POKE 1, &H1E                      ' yellow on blue
    DEF SEG

See Also  \aPEEK\vPEEK\v  \aDEF SEG\vSEG\v  \aVARPTR\vVARPTR\v  \aVARSEG\vVARSEG\v
.context PEN
.topic PEN Function, PEN Statements
The PEN function reads a light pen.  PEN ON, PEN OFF and PEN STOP control
the trapping of the pen by ON PEN GOSUB.

\bPEN\p(n%)
\bPEN ON\p | \bPEN OFF\p | \bPEN STOP\p
\bON PEN GOSUB\p line

    þ n%               What PEN returns:
                         0     -1 if the pen was pressed since the last
                               PEN(0), else 0
                         1, 2  x and y where the pen was last pressed
                         3     -1 if the pen's switch is down, else 0
                         4, 5  the pen's latest x and y
                         6, 7  row and column where it was last pressed
                         8, 9  the pen's latest row and column
    þ line             The label or line number of the handler.

n% outside 0-9 is "Illegal function call".

This QBasic has no light pen: PEN(n%) is always 0 and ON PEN never
happens.

See Also  \aevent trapping\vevents\v  \aON\vON\v  \aSTICK\vSTICK\v
.context PLAY
.topic PLAY Statement, PLAY Function
PLAY plays music on the PC speaker from a string of notes and commands.
The PLAY function returns how many notes are still waiting to be played.

\bPLAY\p commandstring$
\bPLAY\p(n)
\bPLAY ON\p | \bPLAY OFF\p | \bPLAY STOP\p
\bON PLAY\p(queuelimit%) \bGOSUB\p line

    þ commandstring$   The notes and commands: see \aMusic Strings\vPLAY-music\v.
    þ n                Any number; it is not used.
    þ queuelimit%      1 to 32.  The handler runs when fewer notes than
                       this are waiting.
    þ line             The label or line number of the handler.

At first PLAY returns only when its music has finished.  After MB in a
string the music plays in the background while the program goes on, with
up to 32 notes waiting; PLAY(n) counts them (it is 0 in the foreground).
ON PLAY lets the program add more notes before the queue runs dry; PLAY
ON starts that trapping.  See \aevent trapping\vevents\v.

Example:
    PLAY "T160 O3 L8 CDEFGAB > C"
    PLAY "MB L4 C E G"                ' plays while the program goes on
    PRINT PLAY(0); "notes waiting"

See Also  \aMusic Strings\vPLAY-music\v  \aSOUND\vSOUND\v  \aBEEP\vBEEP\v  \aVARPTR$\vVARPTR$\v
.context PLAY-music
.topic PLAY Music Strings
The commands in a PLAY string.  They may be in upper or lower case, and
blanks and semicolons between them are ignored.  Octave, length, tempo and
style carry on from one PLAY to the next.

  \bNotes\p
    A to G        A note in the current octave.  # or + after the letter
                  makes it sharp, - flat.  A length (1-64) may follow,
                  then dots.
    N n           Note n of the whole range, 1 to 84 (seven octaves of
                  twelve).  N0 is a rest.
    P n           A rest of length n (1-64).
    .             After a note or a rest: half as long again.  Each
                  further dot adds half of what the dot before added.

  \bOctaves\p
    O n           The octave, 0 to 6; 4 at the start.  Middle C is the
                  first note of octave 3.
    <  >          One octave down, one octave up.

  \bLength and tempo\p
    L n           The length of the notes that follow: 1 a whole note,
                  4 a quarter (the start), up to 64.
    T n           Quarter notes a minute, 32 to 255; 120 at the start.

  \bStyle\p
    MN            Each note sounds for 7/8 of its length (the start).
    ML            Legato: the whole length.
    MS            Staccato: 3/4 of the length.
    MF            Foreground: PLAY waits until the music has finished.
    MB            Background: the program goes on; up to 32 notes wait.

  \bVariables\p
    X + VARPTR$(a$)   Plays the string a$ here.
    = + VARPTR$(v%)   Any number may be a variable's instead, as in
                      "T=" + VARPTR$(speed%).

A number out of range, or a letter that is no command, is "Illegal
function call".

Example:
    PLAY "T200 L16 O4 CDEC CDEC L8 EFG EFG"
    tune$ = "L8 GFEDC4"
    PLAY "O3 X" + VARPTR$(tune$)

See Also  \aPLAY\vPLAY\v  \aSOUND\vSOUND\v  \aVARPTR$\vVARPTR$\v
.context PMAP
.topic PMAP Function
PMAP converts a coordinate between the units WINDOW set (logical) and the
pixels of the viewport (physical).

\bPMAP\p(coordinate#, n%)

    þ coordinate#      The coordinate to convert.
    þ n%               0  logical x to physical x
                       1  logical y to physical y
                       2  physical x to logical x
                       3  physical y to logical y

Physical coordinates count pixels from the viewport's top left corner.
With no WINDOW set the two kinds are the same.  n% outside 0-3, or PMAP on
a text screen, is "Illegal function call".

Example:
    SCREEN 12
    WINDOW (-10, -10)-(10, 10)
    PRINT PMAP(0, 0); PMAP(0, 1)      ' where (0, 0) is, in pixels

See Also  \aWINDOW\vWINDOW\v  \aVIEW\vVIEW\v  \aPOINT\vPOINT\v
.context POINT
.topic POINT Function
POINT returns the colour of a pixel, or where the last point drawn is.

\bPOINT\p(x!, y!)
\bPOINT\p(n%)

    þ x!, y!           The pixel, in the current coordinates (WINDOW's
                       when one is set).
    þ n%               0  the last point's x, in pixels
                       1  its y, in pixels
                       2  its x in WINDOW's units
                       3  its y in WINDOW's units

POINT(x!, y!) is -1 for a pixel outside the viewport.  Pixels are counted
from the viewport's top left corner; with no WINDOW set, POINT(2) and
POINT(3) are the same as POINT(0) and POINT(1).  POINT on a text screen is
"Illegal function call".

Example:
    SCREEN 13
    PSET (100, 50), 4
    PRINT POINT(100, 50)              ' 4
    PRINT POINT(0); POINT(1)          ' 100  50

See Also  \aPSET\vPSET\v  \aPMAP\vPMAP\v  \aWINDOW\vWINDOW\v  \aVIEW\vVIEW\v
.context POS
.topic POS Function
POS returns the column the cursor is in.

\bPOS\p(n)

    þ n                Any number; it is not used.

The leftmost column is 1.  \aCSRLIN\vCSRLIN\v returns the row.

Example:
    PRINT "Name: ";
    col% = POS(0)                     ' 7

See Also  \aCSRLIN\vCSRLIN\v  \aLOCATE\vLOCATE\v  \aLPOS\vLPOS\v
.context PSET
.context PRESET
.topic PSET, PRESET Statements
PSET sets a pixel to a colour.  PRESET does the same, but without a colour
it uses the background colour, which rubs the pixel out.

\bPSET\p [STEP] (x!, y!) [, colour%]
\bPRESET\p [STEP] (x!, y!) [, colour%]

    þ STEP             x! and y! are offsets from the last point.
    þ x!, y!           The pixel, in the current coordinates.
    þ colour%          The colour.  Without one, PSET uses the
                       foreground colour and PRESET the background (0).

The pixel becomes the last point, which STEP, LINE and DRAW start from.
A pixel outside the viewport is not drawn.  PSET or PRESET on a text
screen is "Illegal function call".

Example:
    SCREEN 12
    FOR x% = 0 TO 639
        PSET (x%, 240 - 100 * SIN(x% / 50)), 10
    NEXT x%

See Also  \aPOINT\vPOINT\v  \aLINE\vLINE\v  \aCIRCLE\vCIRCLE\v  \aCOLOR\vCOLOR\v  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v
.context PRINT
.context LPRINT
.context ?
.topic PRINT, LPRINT Statements
PRINT writes numbers and strings on the screen, or into a file.  LPRINT
writes them on the printer.

\bPRINT\p [#filenumber%,] [expressionlist] [{; | ,}]
\bLPRINT\p [expressionlist] [{; | ,}]

    þ filenumber%      A file opened for OUTPUT or APPEND.  Without
                       one, PRINT writes on the screen.
    þ expressionlist   The items to write, with ; or , between them.
    þ ;                The next item follows straight on.
    þ ,                The next item starts at the next zone of 14
                       columns.

A number is written with a blank before it (or its minus sign) and a blank
after it.  A list that ends with ; or , does not end the line; any other
list does.  PRINT on its own writes an empty line.  An item
that would run past the end of the line goes whole to the next line,
unless it is longer than a line.  \aTAB\vTAB\v and \aSPC\vSPC\v may be put among the
items to move along the line.

? may be typed for PRINT; the editor changes it to PRINT.  LPRINT with no
printer is "Device unavailable".

Example:
    PRINT "Total:"; 12 * 3
    PRINT "Name", "Age"
    OPEN "LOG.TXT" FOR APPEND AS #1
    PRINT #1, DATE$; " "; TIME$
    CLOSE #1

See Also  \aPRINT USING\vUSING\v  \aWRITE\vWRITE\v  \aWIDTH\vWIDTH\v  \aLOCATE\vLOCATE\v  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v
.context USING
.topic PRINT USING, LPRINT USING Statements
PRINT USING writes numbers and strings laid out by a format string.
LPRINT USING does the same on the printer.

\bPRINT\p [#filenumber%,] \bUSING\p format$; expressionlist [{; | ,}]
\bLPRINT USING\p format$; expressionlist [{; | ,}]

    þ filenumber%      A file opened for OUTPUT or APPEND.
    þ format$          Text with fields in it (see below).
    þ expressionlist   The values, with ; or , between them; here the
                       two mean the same.  A ; or , at the end leaves
                       the cursor on the line.

Each value takes the format's text up to the next field, then fills the
field.  When the format runs out it starts again from the left.  After
the last value, the text up to the next field is written too.

  \bNumber fields\p
    #        A digit.
    .        The decimal point: ##.## writes two decimals, rounded.
    ,        Before the point: the digits in groups of three, with commas.
    +        At the start or the end: the number's sign, + or -.
    -        At the end: a minus after a negative number.
    $$       A dollar sign just before the first digit.
    **       The blanks in front of the number filled with asterisks.
    **$      Both of these.
    ^^^^     An exponent, E+nn (D+nn for a DOUBLE); ^^^^^ gives E+nnn.

  \bString fields\p
    !        The string's first character.
    &        The whole string.
    \\   \\    As many characters as the field is wide, both backslashes
             counted; each blank between them adds one.

  \bOthers\p
    _        Writes the next character as it is: _# writes #.

A number too big for its field is written whole, with % in front of it.
A value of the wrong kind for its field is "Type mismatch"; a format with
no field in it, or a number field over 24 characters, is "Illegal
function call".

Example:
    PRINT USING "##.##"; 3.14159              ' 3.14
    PRINT USING "$$#,###.##"; 1234.5          ' $1,234.50
    PRINT USING "\\  \\ is ##"; "Annette"; 7    ' Anne is  7

See Also  \aPRINT\vPRINT\v  \aWRITE\vWRITE\v  \aSTR$\vSTR$\v  \aPALETTE USING\vPALETTE\v
.context RANDOMIZE
.topic RANDOMIZE Statement
RANDOMIZE starts the random-number generator at a new place, so that RND
gives a different series of numbers.

\bRANDOMIZE\p [seed]

    þ seed             Any number.  Without one, RANDOMIZE asks for a
                       seed from -32768 to 32767 on the screen.

Every run starts the generator in the same state, so a program without
RANDOMIZE gets the same numbers from RND each time.  RANDOMIZE TIMER takes
the seed from the clock, which differs from run to run.

The seed changes only part of the generator's state, so the same seed
does not always give the same series.  To repeat a series, use RND(-1)
before each RANDOMIZE.

Example:
    RANDOMIZE TIMER
    die% = INT(RND * 6) + 1
    PRINT "You threw a"; die%

See Also  \aRND\vRND\v  \aTIMER\vTIMER\v
.context READ
.topic READ Statement
READ takes items from the program's DATA statements into variables.

\bREAD\p variablelist

    þ variablelist     Variables, separated by commas.  Each takes the
                       next DATA item.

The items are taken in order, from the first DATA statement in the program
to the last.  DATA belongs to the module-level code, and a READ in a SUB or
FUNCTION reads the same items.  \aRESTORE\vRESTORE\v sets where reading goes on.

A string variable takes any item, as text.  A numeric variable needs an
item that is a number (an empty item reads as 0): anything else is
"Syntax error", shown at the DATA statement.  A number too big for the
variable is "Overflow".  Reading past the last item is "Out of DATA".

Example:
    FOR i% = 1 TO 3
        READ who$, age%
        PRINT who$; age%
    NEXT i%
    DATA Ann, 34, Bob, 27, "Smith, J", 51

See Also  \aDATA\vDATA\v  \aRESTORE\vRESTORE\v
.context REM
.topic REM Statement
REM makes the rest of the line a remark, which QBasic skips when the
program runs.

\bREM\p remark
\b'\p remark

    þ remark           Any text, up to the end of the line.

An apostrophe (') does the same as REM, and can follow a statement
directly; after a statement, REM needs a colon before it.  The
metacommands $DYNAMIC and $STATIC are written in remarks: see
\aMetacommands\vmeta\v.

Example:
    REM The area of a circle
    r = 5                             ' the radius
    area = 3.141593 * r ^ 2: REM pi r squared

See Also  \aMetacommands\vmeta\v
.context RESET
.topic RESET Statement
RESET closes every open file and device, and writes out what is waiting
in their buffers.

\bRESET\p

RESET does the same as CLOSE with no file numbers.  The printer that
LPRINT uses is closed too.

Example:
    OPEN "A.TXT" FOR OUTPUT AS #1
    OPEN "B.TXT" FOR OUTPUT AS #2
    RESET                             ' both files are closed

See Also  \aCLOSE\vCLOSE\v  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v  \aEND\vEND\v
.context RESTORE
.topic RESTORE Statement
RESTORE sets which DATA item the next READ takes.

\bRESTORE\p [line]

    þ line             A line number or label.  The next READ takes the
                       first item of the first DATA statement at or
                       after it.  Without one, the first DATA item in
                       the program.

Example:
    READ a%, b%
    RESTORE
    READ c%                           ' c% is 10, the same as a%
    DATA 10, 20

See Also  \aREAD\vREAD\v  \aDATA\vDATA\v
.context RESUME
.topic RESUME Statement
RESUME ends an error handler and says where the program goes on.

\bRESUME\p [0]
\bRESUME NEXT\p
\bRESUME\p line

    þ 0                The same as RESUME alone: the statement that
                       failed runs again.
    þ NEXT             The program goes on with the statement after
                       the one that failed.
    þ line             The program goes on at this line number or
                       label.

ERR goes back to 0.  RESUME when no error is being handled is "RESUME
without error".

Example:
    ON ERROR GOTO NoFile
    OPEN "DATA.TXT" FOR INPUT AS #1
    END
    NoFile:
    OPEN "DATA.TXT" FOR OUTPUT AS #1: CLOSE #1   ' make an empty one
    RESUME                                       ' and try again

See Also  \aON ERROR\vON-ERROR\v  \aERR\vERR\v  \aERL\vERL\v  \aERROR\vERROR\v  \aRun-Time Error Codes\verrcodes\v
.context RND
.topic RND Function
RND returns a random number from 0 up to, but not including, 1.

\bRND\p[(n!)]

    þ n!               Above 0, or left out: the next number of the
                       series.  0: the last number again.  Below 0: a
                       number that depends on n! alone, and a new
                       series that starts from it.

The series is the same in every run unless the program uses RANDOMIZE.
A whole number from low% to high% is INT(RND * (high% - low% + 1)) + low%.

Example:
    RANDOMIZE TIMER
    FOR i% = 1 TO 5
        PRINT INT(RND * 100) + 1;     ' five numbers from 1 to 100
    NEXT i%

See Also  \aRANDOMIZE\vRANDOMIZE\v  \aINT\vINT\v
.context RUN
.topic RUN Statement
RUN starts the program again from the beginning, or loads another program
and runs that.

\bRUN\p [{linenumber | file$}]

    þ linenumber       The line of the module to start at.
    þ file$            The program to load and run.  .BAS is added to
                       a name that has no extension.

RUN closes every file and clears every variable before it starts.  With
file$, the program in memory is replaced; QBasic asks first whether to
save changes to it.  A file$ that is not there is "File not found", and a
linenumber the module does not have is "Label not defined".

Example:
    INPUT "Again (Y/N)"; a$
    IF UCASE$(a$) = "Y" THEN RUN
    RUN "MENU"                        ' loads and runs MENU.BAS

See Also  \aCHAIN\vCHAIN\v  \aEND\vEND\v  \aSYSTEM\vSYSTEM\v  \aCLEAR\vCLEAR\v
.context SADD
.topic SADD Function
SADD returns the offset of the characters of a string variable.

\bSADD\p(stringvariable$)

    þ stringvariable$  A variable-length string.  A fixed-length string
                       is "Type mismatch": use VARPTR for one.

The characters are in the default data segment, the one DEF SEG sets
when it is given no segment.  The offset is an INTEGER, so one above
32767 comes out negative.  It holds only until the string is changed: a
new value is kept somewhere else.

Example:
    a$ = "Hello"
    DEF SEG                           ' the default data segment
    PRINT CHR$(PEEK(SADD(a$)))        ' H

See Also  \aVARPTR\vVARPTR\v  \aVARSEG\vVARSEG\v  \aPEEK\vPEEK\v  \aDEF SEG\vSEG\v
.context SCREEN
.topic SCREEN Statement
SCREEN sets the screen mode, and which page the program writes on and
which page is shown.

\bSCREEN\p [mode%] [, [colourswitch%] [, [activepage%] [, visualpage%]]]

    þ mode%            The screen mode: see \aScreen Modes\vscreenmodes\v.
    þ colourswitch%    Colour on or off on old CGA monitors.  It
                       changes nothing on an EGA or VGA.
    þ activepage%      The page PRINT and the drawing statements write
                       on.
    þ visualpage%      The page shown.  Without one, the active page.

A different mode clears the screen, sets its text size, and puts back its
colours and palette; VIEW and WINDOW are turned off and both pages are 0.
A mode or a page the screen does not have is "Illegal function call".

Drawing on a page that is not shown, and then showing it (or copying it
with PCOPY), changes the whole picture at once.

SCREEN 3 and 4 (Hercules, Olivetti) are not in this QBasic.

Example:
    SCREEN 12                         ' 640 x 480, 16 colours
    CIRCLE (320, 240), 100, 14
    SLEEP
    SCREEN 0                          ' back to text
    WIDTH 80, 25

See Also  \aScreen Modes\vscreenmodes\v  \aSCREEN function\vSCREEN-function\v  \aPCOPY\vPCOPY\v
          \aCOLOR\vCOLOR\v  \aPALETTE\vPALETTE\v  \aWIDTH\vWIDTH\v  \aVIEW\vVIEW\v  \aWINDOW\vWINDOW\v
.context screenmodes
.topic Screen Modes
The modes SCREEN sets.  Attributes are the colour numbers a program draws
with; colours are how many PALETTE can choose from for them.

  Mode  Pixels    Text columns x rows     Attributes  Colours  Pages
    0   -         40 or 80 x 25, 43, 50       16          64   0-7
    1   320x200   40 x 25                      4          16   0
    2   640x200   80 x 25                      2          16   0
    7   320x200   40 x 25                     16          16   0-7
    8   640x200   80 x 25                     16          16   0-3
    9   640x350   80 x 25 or 43               16          64   0-1
   10   640x350   80 x 25 or 43                4           9   0-1
   11   640x480   80 x 30 or 60                2     262,144   0
   12   640x480   80 x 30 or 60               16     262,144   0
   13   320x200   40 x 25                    256     262,144   0

SCREEN 0 is text only; with 43 or 50 rows of 80 columns it has pages 0-3.
SCREEN 10 is for a monochrome monitor.  WIDTH chooses between the numbers
of rows.  Characters are 8 pixels wide; they are 8 high, but 14 in
SCREEN 9 and 10 at 25 rows and 16 in SCREEN 11 and 12 at 30 rows.

QBasic needs an EGA for modes 7 to 10 and a VGA for 11 to 13.  This
QBasic shows every graphics mode through a VGA mode, so it needs a VGA for
all of them.

See Also  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v  \aWIDTH\vWIDTH\v  \aPALETTE\vPALETTE\v  \aCOLOR\vCOLOR\v  \aPCOPY\vPCOPY\v
.context SCREEN-function
.topic SCREEN Function
The SCREEN function returns the code of the character at a place on the
screen, or its colour.

\bSCREEN\p(row%, column% [, colourflag%])

    þ row%, column%    The place.  The top left corner is 1, 1.
    þ colourflag%      Not 0: return the character's colour attribute
                       instead of its code.

The attribute is the foreground colour + 16 * the background colour, + 128
when the character blinks.  A place off the screen is "Illegal function
call".

In a graphics mode this QBasic returns the character PRINT put at the
place, and 0 for its colour.

Example:
    CLS
    PRINT "Hello"
    PRINT CHR$(SCREEN(1, 1))          ' H
    PRINT SCREEN(1, 1, 1)             ' 7: grey on black

See Also  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v  \aCSRLIN\vCSRLIN\v  \aPOS\vPOS\v  \aCOLOR\vCOLOR\v
.context SEEK
.topic SEEK Function and Statement
The SEEK function returns where in a file the next read or write will
happen.  The SEEK statement sets the place.

\bSEEK\p(filenumber%)
\bSEEK\p [#]filenumber%, position&

    þ filenumber%      The number of an open file.
    þ position&        In a RANDOM file, a record number; in other
                       files, a byte number.  The first is 1.

The SEEK function returns the next record of a RANDOM file, or the next
byte of any other.  A position past the end of the file is allowed: a
write there makes the file longer.  A position below 1 is "Bad record
number".  A GET or PUT with a record number moves the position too.

Example:
    OPEN "SCORES.DAT" FOR BINARY AS #1
    SEEK #1, LOF(1) + 1               ' to the end of the file
    PUT #1, , total&
    PRINT SEEK(1)                     ' the byte after total&
    CLOSE #1

See Also  \aGET\vGET\v  \aPUT\vPUT\v  \aLOC\vLOC\v  \aLOF\vLOF\v  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v
.context SELECT
.context CASE
.context IS
.topic SELECT CASE Statement
SELECT CASE runs one of several blocks of statements, chosen by the value
of an expression.

\bSELECT CASE\p testexpression
\bCASE\p expressionlist1
    [statementblock-1]
[\bCASE\p expressionlist2
    [statementblock-2]]...
[\bCASE ELSE\p
    [statementblock-n]]
\bEND SELECT\p

    þ testexpression   A number or a string.
    þ expressionlist   One or more of these, with commas between:
                         expression
                         expression \bTO\p expression
                         \bIS\p relational-operator expression
    þ statementblock   The statements to run when the list matches.

The CASEs are tried in order.  The first one whose list matches the value
runs its block, and the program goes on after END SELECT.  CASE ELSE, if
there is one, comes last and runs when no other CASE matches.

In a TO range the smaller value comes first.  IS takes =, <>, <, <=, >
or >=.  Strings compare by their character codes.  When the test value is
an INTEGER or LONG, a CASE value with a fraction is rounded first.

Example:
    SELECT CASE score%
        CASE IS < 0, IS > 100: PRINT "Not a score"
        CASE 90 TO 100: PRINT "A"
        CASE 50 TO 89: PRINT "Pass"
        CASE ELSE: PRINT "Fail"
    END SELECT

See Also  \aIF\vIF\v  \aON...GOTO\vON-GOTO\v  \aBoolean Operators\vboolean\v
.context SGN
.topic SGN Function
SGN returns the sign of a number: -1 if it is negative, 0 if it is zero,
1 if it is positive.

\bSGN\p(numericexpression)

Example:
    PRINT SGN(-4.5); SGN(0); SGN(17)  ' -1  0  1

See Also  \aABS\vABS\v
.context SHARED
.topic SHARED Statement
SHARED, in a SUB or FUNCTION, gives the procedure the module-level
variables of the names it lists.

\bSHARED\p variable[()] [AS type] [, variable[()] [AS type]]...

    þ variable         A module-level variable, or an array with empty
                       brackets.
    þ AS type          The variable's type, as the module declares it.

Without SHARED, a procedure's variables are its own, even when the module
has one of the same name.  DIM SHARED in the module shares a variable with
every procedure at once.  SHARED outside a SUB or FUNCTION is an error.

Example:
    CALL AddUp(5)
    PRINT total&                      ' 5
    SUB AddUp (n%)
    SHARED total&
    total& = total& + n%
    END SUB

See Also  \aDIM\vDIM\v  \aSTATIC\vSTATIC\v  \aSUB\vSUB\v  \aFUNCTION\vFUNCTION\v  \aCOMMON\vCOMMON\v
.context SHELL
.topic SHELL Statement
SHELL runs a DOS command, or a command prompt, and then comes back to the
program.

\bSHELL\p [commandstring$]

    þ commandstring$   The command, as it would be typed at the prompt.
                       Without one, a prompt appears; EXIT at the
                       prompt comes back.

The program waits until the command has finished.  Its files stay open,
and the command gets the environment as ENVIRON has left it.
A command interpreter that cannot be found is "File not found".

SHELL runs the PM-DOS command interpreter that COMSPEC names, or CMD.EXE
when there is no COMSPEC.

Example:
    SHELL "DIR *.BAS /W"
    SHELL                             ' a prompt: type EXIT to come back

See Also  \aRUN\vRUN\v  \aCHAIN\vCHAIN\v  \aENVIRON$\vENVIRON$\v
.context SIN
.topic SIN Function
SIN returns the sine of an angle.

\bSIN\p(angle)

    þ angle            The angle in radians.  Degrees times 3.141593 /
                       180 gives radians.

The result is a SINGLE, or a DOUBLE when the angle is one.  This QBasic
works SIN out on the coprocessor in its full precision.

Example:
    deg = 30
    PRINT SIN(deg * 3.141593 / 180)   ' about .5

See Also  \aCOS\vCOS\v  \aTAN\vTAN\v  \aATN\vATN\v
.context SLEEP
.topic SLEEP Statement
SLEEP stops the program until a key is pressed, a number of seconds has
gone by, or an event that is being trapped (ON TIMER, ON PLAY) happens.

\bSLEEP\p [seconds&]

    þ seconds&         How long to wait.  Without it, or with 0, SLEEP
                       waits for a key however long that takes.

The key that ends SLEEP stays in the keyboard buffer, for INKEY$ or INPUT
to take.

Example:
    PRINT "Press a key, or wait 5 seconds"
    SLEEP 5
    k$ = INKEY$                       ' the key, if one was pressed

See Also  \aINKEY$\vINKEY$\v  \aTIMER\vTIMER\v  \aWAIT\vWAIT\v
.context SOUND
.topic SOUND Statement
SOUND makes a tone on the PC speaker.

\bSOUND\p frequency, duration

    þ frequency        The pitch in hertz, 37 to 32767.
    þ duration         The length in clock ticks, 0 to 65535.  There
                       are 18.2 ticks in a second.

The program waits until the tone has finished, unless PLAY "MB" has set
background music: then the tone joins the queue of notes.  A duration of 0
ends any sound being made.  A number out of range is "Illegal function
call".

Example:
    FOR f% = 200 TO 800 STEP 100
        SOUND f%, 2
    NEXT f%

See Also  \aPLAY\vPLAY\v  \aBEEP\vBEEP\v
.context SPACE$
.topic SPACE$ Function
SPACE$ returns a string of blanks.

\bSPACE$\p(n%)

    þ n%               How many blanks, 0 to 32767.

Example:
    PRINT "A"; SPACE$(10); "B"

See Also  \aSPC\vSPC\v  \aSTRING$\vSTRING$\v  \aTAB\vTAB\v
.context SPC
.topic SPC Function
SPC, among the items of a PRINT, LPRINT or PRINT #, writes blanks.

\bSPC\p(n%)

    þ n%               How many blanks.  Only the remainder after n% is
                       divided by the width of the line counts.

SPC counts from where the cursor is; TAB moves to a column.  SPC can be
used only in PRINT, LPRINT and PRINT #.

Example:
    PRINT "Left"; SPC(10); "Right"

See Also  \aTAB\vTAB\v  \aSPACE$\vSPACE$\v  \aPRINT\vPRINT\v
.context SQR
.topic SQR Function
SQR returns the square root of a number.

\bSQR\p(n)

    þ n                0 or more.  A negative number is "Illegal
                       function call".

Example:
    PRINT SQR(2)                      ' 1.414214
    side = SQR(a ^ 2 + b ^ 2)

See Also  \aArithmetic Operators\varith\v  \aEXP\vEXP\v  \aLOG\vLOG\v
.context STATIC
.topic STATIC Statement
STATIC, in a SUB, FUNCTION or DEF FN, makes variables that belong to the
procedure alone and keep their values from one call to the next.

\bSTATIC\p variable[()] [AS type] [, variable[()] [AS type]]...

    þ variable         A variable, or an array with empty brackets (DIM
                       gives its size after the STATIC).
    þ AS type          The variable's type.

A procedure's other variables start again at 0 or "" on every call.  A
STATIC variable is the procedure's own even when the module shares one of
the same name.  STATIC at the end of a SUB or FUNCTION line makes all of
the procedure's variables keep their values.  STATIC outside a procedure
is an error.  The $STATIC metacommand is another matter: see
\aMetacommands\vmeta\v.

Example:
    SUB Counter
        STATIC calls%
        calls% = calls% + 1
        PRINT "Called"; calls%; "times"
    END SUB

See Also  \aSHARED\vSHARED\v  \aSUB\vSUB\v  \aFUNCTION\vFUNCTION\v  \aDIM\vDIM\v
.context STICK
.topic STICK Function
STICK returns a joystick's position.

\bSTICK\p(n%)

    þ n%               0  joystick A's x
                       1  joystick A's y
                       2  joystick B's x
                       3  joystick B's y

STICK(0) reads both joysticks; STICK(1) to STICK(3) return what that
reading found, so STICK(0) comes first.  The numbers grow as a stick moves
right or down.  n% outside 0-3 is "Illegal function call".

Example:
    DO
        x% = STICK(0): y% = STICK(1)
        LOCATE 1, 1: PRINT x%, y%
    LOOP UNTIL INKEY$ <> ""

See Also  \aSTRIG\vSTRIG\v
.context STOP
.topic STOP Statement
STOP halts the program and goes back to the editor, with the STOP
statement lit.  F5 goes on from the statement after it.

\bSTOP\p

STOP is for finding mistakes: while the program is stopped, the Immediate
window can show and change its variables.  Files stay open.  To end a
program, use END.

Example:
    FOR i% = 1 TO 10
        total = total + i%
        IF total > 20 THEN STOP       ' look at i% and total here
    NEXT i%

See Also  \aEND\vEND\v  \aSYSTEM\vSYSTEM\v  \aRun and Debug Keys\vdebugkeys\v
.context STR$
.topic STR$ Function
STR$ returns a number as a string, the way PRINT writes it.

\bSTR$\p(numericexpression)

A number that is not negative gets a blank in front, where a minus sign
would go.  There is no blank after it.  LTRIM$ takes the front blank off.

Example:
    a$ = STR$(42)                     ' " 42"
    PRINT "[" + LTRIM$(STR$(42)) + "]"   ' [42]

See Also  \aVAL\vVAL\v  \aLTRIM$\vLTRIM$\v  \aPRINT USING\vUSING\v  \aHEX$\vHEX$\v
.context STRIG
.topic STRIG Function and Statement
The STRIG function reads the joystick buttons.  STRIG ON, OFF and STOP
control the trapping of a button by ON STRIG GOSUB.

\bSTRIG\p(n%)
\bSTRIG\p(button%) \bON\p | \bSTRIG\p(button%) \bOFF\p | \bSTRIG\p(button%) \bSTOP\p
\bON STRIG\p(button%) \bGOSUB\p line

    þ n%               What STRIG returns, -1 for yes and 0 for no:
                         0     was A's first button pressed since the
                               last STRIG(0)?
                         1     is A's first button down?
                         2, 3  the same for B's first button
                         4, 5  the same for A's second button
                         6, 7  the same for B's second button
    þ button%          0, 2, 4 or 6: the button, numbered as above.
    þ line             The label or line number of the handler.

n% outside 0-7 is "Illegal function call".

In this QBasic every STRIG(n%) says whether the button is down now, and
ON STRIG never happens.

Example:
    DO
        IF STRIG(1) THEN PRINT "Fire!"
    LOOP UNTIL INKEY$ <> ""

See Also  \aSTICK\vSTICK\v  \aevent trapping\vevents\v  \aON\vON\v
.context STRING$
.topic STRING$ Function
STRING$ returns a string of one character repeated.

\bSTRING$\p(length%, {code% | string$})

    þ length%          How many characters, 0 to 32767.
    þ code%            The character's code: see \aASCII Character Codes\vascii\v.
    þ string$          A string whose first character is repeated.  An
                       empty one is "Illegal function call".

Example:
    PRINT STRING$(40, "-")
    PRINT STRING$(10, 177)

See Also  \aSPACE$\vSPACE$\v  \aCHR$\vCHR$\v  \aASCII Character Codes\vascii\v
.context SUB
.topic SUB Statement
SUB begins a procedure: a block of statements with a name and variables of
its own, which CALL runs.

\bSUB\p name [(parameterlist)] [\bSTATIC\p]
    [statementblock]
    [\bEXIT SUB\p]
    [statementblock]
\bEND SUB\p

    þ name             The procedure's name, with no type suffix.
    þ parameterlist    The variables that take the arguments, with
                       commas between: name[()] [AS type].  Empty
                       brackets mark an array.
    þ STATIC           The procedure's variables keep their values
                       from one call to the next.

CALL name(arguments) runs the SUB, and so does its name with the arguments
after it and no brackets.  A variable given as an argument is passed by
reference: when the SUB changes the parameter, the variable changes.  An
expression, or a variable in brackets, is passed as a copy.  EXIT SUB
leaves early.  A SUB can call itself.

The editor keeps each SUB in a window of its own: \aNew SUB\vm.newsub\v on the
Edit menu starts one, and \aSUBs\vm.subs\v (F2) lists them.  Saving the
program puts a DECLARE for each SUB at the top of the module.

Example:
    CALL Box(5, 10)
    SUB Box (row%, col%)
        LOCATE row%, col%: PRINT "+--+"
        LOCATE row% + 1, col%: PRINT "+--+"
    END SUB

See Also  \aCALL\vCALL\v  \aFUNCTION\vFUNCTION\v  \aDECLARE\vDECLARE\v  \aSHARED\vSHARED\v  \aSTATIC\vSTATIC\v  \aEXIT\vEXIT\v
.context SWAP
.topic SWAP Statement
SWAP exchanges the values of two variables.

\bSWAP\p variable1, variable2

The two must be of the same type, or it is "Type mismatch".  They may be
array elements or records.

Example:
    IF a% > b% THEN SWAP a%, b%       ' now a% <= b%

See Also  \aLET\vLET\v
.context SYSTEM
.topic SYSTEM Statement
SYSTEM ends the program and closes its files.  A program started with
QBASIC /RUN goes back to DOS; one run from the editor goes back to the
editor.

\bSYSTEM\p

Typed in the Immediate window, SYSTEM leaves QBasic.

Example:
    INPUT "Quit (Y/N)"; a$
    IF UCASE$(a$) = "Y" THEN SYSTEM

See Also  \aEND\vEND\v  \aSTOP\vSTOP\v  \aQBasic Command Line\vcmdline\v
.context TAB
.topic TAB Function
TAB, among the items of a PRINT, LPRINT or PRINT #, moves to a column.

\bTAB\p(column%)

    þ column%          The column to move to; the leftmost is 1.

When the cursor is already past the column, TAB goes to that column of
the next line.  On the screen and the printer, a column beyond the width
counts as the remainder after it is divided by the width; a column below
1 counts as 1.  TAB can be used only in PRINT, LPRINT and PRINT #.

Example:
    PRINT "Name"; TAB(20); "Phone"
    PRINT "Ann"; TAB(20); "555-0134"

See Also  \aSPC\vSPC\v  \aPRINT\vPRINT\v  \aLOCATE\vLOCATE\v
.context TAN
.topic TAN Function
TAN returns the tangent of an angle.

\bTAN\p(angle)

    þ angle            The angle in radians.

The result is a SINGLE, or a DOUBLE when the angle is one.

Example:
    PRINT TAN(3.141593 / 4)           ' about 1: 45 degrees

See Also  \aSIN\vSIN\v  \aCOS\vCOS\v  \aATN\vATN\v
.context TIME$
.topic TIME$ Function and Statement
The TIME$ function returns the time of day.  The TIME$ statement sets the
computer's clock.

\bTIME$\p
\bTIME$\p = timestring$

    þ timestring$      The new time: "hh", "hh:mm" or "hh:mm:ss", on a
                       24-hour clock.

TIME$ returns eight characters, hh:mm:ss, on a 24-hour clock.  A
timestring$ that is not a time is "Illegal function call".

Example:
    PRINT "The time is "; TIME$       ' 14:05:09, say
    TIME$ = "08:30"

See Also  \aDATE$\vDATE$\v  \aTIMER\vTIMER\v
.context TIMER
.topic TIMER Function, ON TIMER
The TIMER function returns the number of seconds since midnight.  ON TIMER
runs a subroutine every so many seconds.

\bTIMER\p
\bTIMER ON\p | \bTIMER OFF\p | \bTIMER STOP\p
\bON TIMER\p(seconds%) \bGOSUB\p line

    þ seconds%         How often the handler runs: 1 to 86400 seconds.
    þ line             The label or line number of the handler, which
                       ends with RETURN.

TIMER is a SINGLE.  It moves on about 18 times a second and goes back to 0
at midnight.  The difference between two readings times part of a
program.

ON TIMER names the handler and TIMER ON starts the trapping.  TIMER OFF
stops it; after TIMER STOP the handler waits until the next TIMER ON.  See
\aevent trapping\vevents\v.

Example:
    ON TIMER(1) GOSUB Clock
    TIMER ON
    DO: LOOP UNTIL INKEY$ <> ""
    END
    Clock: LOCATE 1, 70: PRINT TIME$: RETURN

See Also  \aTIME$\vTIME$\v  \aRANDOMIZE\vRANDOMIZE\v  \aSLEEP\vSLEEP\v  \aevent trapping\vevents\v
.context TRON
.context TROFF
.topic TRON, TROFF Statements
TRON turns tracing on: the editor lights each statement as the program
runs it.  TROFF turns tracing off.

\bTRON\p
\bTROFF\p

They do what \aTrace On\vm.trace\v on the Debug menu does, from inside the
program.

Example:
    TRON
    FOR i% = 1 TO 3: PRINT i%: NEXT i%
    TROFF

See Also  \aTrace On\vm.trace\v  \aSTOP\vSTOP\v
.context TYPE
.topic TYPE Statement
TYPE defines a record type: named elements kept together in one variable.

\bTYPE\p usertype
    elementname \bAS\p typename
    [elementname \bAS\p typename]
    ...
\bEND TYPE\p

    þ usertype         The name of the new type.
    þ elementname      The name of an element.
    þ typename         INTEGER, LONG, SINGLE, DOUBLE, STRING * n (a
                       fixed-length string), or a type defined earlier.

DIM name AS usertype makes a variable of the type, and name.elementname
is one of its elements.  A record has a fixed size, which LEN returns, so
records suit RANDOM files with GET and PUT.  An element cannot be an
array or a variable-length string.  TYPE goes in the module-level code,
not in a procedure.

Example:
    TYPE Person
        surname AS STRING * 20
        age AS INTEGER
    END TYPE
    DIM p AS Person
    p.surname = "Smith": p.age = 42

See Also  \aDIM\vDIM\v  \aGET\vGET\v  \aPUT\vPUT\v  \aLEN\vLEN\v  \aData Types\vtypes\v
.context UEVENT
.topic UEVENT Keyword
UEVENT belongs to the trapping of events a program defines itself, which
QBasic does not have.  The words are reserved for ON UEVENT GOSUB and
UEVENT ON, OFF and STOP.

In this QBasic a statement that starts with UEVENT is "Advanced feature
unavailable".

See Also  \aevent trapping\vevents\v
.context VAL
.topic VAL Function
VAL returns the number at the start of a string.

\bVAL\p(stringexpression$)

VAL reads from the left.  Blanks are skipped, even between digits, and
the reading stops at the first character that cannot be part of the
number.  A string that does not start with a number gives 0.  &H and &O
in front read hexadecimal and octal numbers, and an exponent with E or D
is understood.  A number too big for a DOUBLE is "Overflow".

Example:
    PRINT VAL("  12.5 kg")            ' 12.5
    PRINT VAL("1 000")                ' 1000
    PRINT VAL("&HFF")                 ' 255
    PRINT VAL("abc")                  ' 0

See Also  \aSTR$\vSTR$\v  \aINPUT\vINPUT\v
.context VARPTR
.context VARSEG
.topic VARPTR, VARSEG Functions
VARPTR returns the offset of a variable in memory, and VARSEG its segment.
The two together are its address.

\bVARPTR\p(variable)
\bVARSEG\p(variable)

    þ variable         Any variable, array element or record.

Both are INTEGERs, so a value above 32767 comes out negative.  For a
variable-length string the address is that of its descriptor: two bytes
of length, then the offset of the characters (see \aSADD\vSADD\v).  Arrays and
strings can move while the program runs, so take an address just before
it is used.

These addresses are for the program's own PEEK, POKE, BLOAD and BSAVE;
there is no real-mode memory behind them.  See \aQBasic on PM-DOS\vpmdos\v.

Example:
    DIM a%(10)
    a%(3) = 258
    DEF SEG = VARSEG(a%(3))
    PRINT PEEK(VARPTR(a%(3))); PEEK(VARPTR(a%(3)) + 1)   ' 2  1
    DEF SEG

See Also  \aVARPTR$\vVARPTR$\v  \aSADD\vSADD\v  \aPEEK\vPEEK\v  \aPOKE\vPOKE\v  \aDEF SEG\vSEG\v
.context VARPTR$
.topic VARPTR$ Function
VARPTR$ returns a variable's address as a string of 3 bytes, for DRAW and
PLAY strings.

\bVARPTR$\p(variable)

    þ variable         A string variable, for X in DRAW and PLAY, or a
                       numeric variable, for a number given as = in the
                       string.

The first byte gives the variable's type and the other two its address.
The string means something only to DRAW and PLAY.

Example:
    side% = 40
    DRAW "U=" + VARPTR$(side%) + "R=" + VARPTR$(side%)
    tune$ = "CDE"
    PLAY "X" + VARPTR$(tune$)

See Also  \aDRAW\vDRAW\v  \aPLAY\vPLAY\v  \aMusic Strings\vPLAY-music\v  \aVARPTR\vVARPTR\v
.context VIEW
.topic VIEW Statement
VIEW sets the viewport: the part of a graphics screen that drawing
reaches.

\bVIEW\p [[SCREEN] (x1!, y1!)-(x2!, y2!) [, [fill%] [, border%]]]

    þ SCREEN           Points stay in screen coordinates; drawing is
                       only cut off at the viewport's edges.  Without
                       SCREEN, (0, 0) is the viewport's top left corner.
    þ (x1!, y1!)       One corner, in pixels of the whole screen.
    þ (x2!, y2!)       The opposite corner.
    þ fill%            A colour to fill the viewport with.
    þ border%          A colour for a line round the viewport, just
                       outside it.

VIEW alone makes the whole screen the viewport again.  A corner off the
screen, or VIEW on a text screen, is "Illegal function call".  CLS clears
only the viewport while one is set.  A WINDOW stays when the viewport
changes, its units fitted to the new one.  \aVIEW PRINT\vVIEW-PRINT\v sets
the area for text.

Example:
    SCREEN 12
    VIEW (100, 100)-(539, 379), 1, 15     ' a blue box, a white edge
    CIRCLE (220, 140), 180, 14            ' cut off at the box's edges
    VIEW                                  ' the whole screen again

See Also  \aWINDOW\vWINDOW\v  \aVIEW PRINT\vVIEW-PRINT\v  \aCLS\vCLS\v  \aPMAP\vPMAP\v  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v
.context VIEW-PRINT
.topic VIEW PRINT Statement
VIEW PRINT sets the rows of the screen that text is printed and scrolled
in.

\bVIEW PRINT\p [toprow% \bTO\p bottomrow%]

    þ toprow%          The first row of the area.
    þ bottomrow%       The last row of the area.

Rows outside the area stay as they are while the area scrolls.  The cursor
goes to the start of the top row.  LOCATE can then reach only the rows of
the area, and CLS clears only the area.  VIEW PRINT alone puts back the
whole screen, whose bottom row never scrolls.  Rows the screen does not
have are "Illegal function call".

Example:
    CLS
    PRINT "Title line"
    VIEW PRINT 3 TO 20
    FOR i% = 1 TO 100: PRINT i%: NEXT i%   ' the title stays put
    VIEW PRINT

See Also  \aVIEW\vVIEW\v  \aCLS\vCLS\v  \aLOCATE\vLOCATE\v  \aWIDTH\vWIDTH\v
.context WAIT
.topic WAIT Statement
WAIT stops the program until an input port shows a bit pattern.

\bWAIT\p port%, and% [, xor%]

    þ port%            The port number, 0 to 65535.
    þ and%             The bits to watch, 0 to 255.
    þ xor%             Bits to turn over first, 0 to 255, so that WAIT
                       can wait for a bit to be 0.  Without it, 0.

WAIT reads the port over and over until (INP(port%) XOR xor%) AND and% is
not 0.  If that never happens the program waits until Ctrl+Break.

The ports are the hardware's, except the keyboard controller's and A20's
(&H60, &H64 and &H92): see \aINP\vINP\v.

Example:
    WAIT &H3DA, 8                     ' until the vertical retrace

See Also  \aINP\vINP\v  \aOUT\vOUT\v
.context WHILE
.context WEND
.topic WHILE...WEND Statement
WHILE...WEND runs a block of statements again and again while a condition
is true.

\bWHILE\p condition
    [statementblock]
\bWEND\p

    þ condition        A numeric expression; anything but 0 is true.

The condition is tested before each pass, so the block may not run at
all.  DO...LOOP does the same and more, and EXIT DO can leave it; there
is no statement to leave a WHILE loop.

Example:
    n% = 1
    WHILE n% < 1000
        n% = n% * 2
    WEND
    PRINT n%                          ' 1024

See Also  \aDO\vDO\v  \aFOR\vFOR\v
.context WIDTH
.topic WIDTH Statement
WIDTH sets the columns and rows of the screen, or the line width of a
file, a device or the printer.

\bWIDTH\p [columns%] [, rows%]
\bWIDTH\p {#filenumber% | device$}, columns%
\bWIDTH LPRINT\p columns%

    þ columns%         On the screen, 40 or 80.  For a file, device or
                       printer, the line width, 0 to 255; 255 means no
                       limit.
    þ rows%            25, 43 or 50 in SCREEN 0.  For the graphics
                       modes see \aScreen Modes\vscreenmodes\v.
    þ filenumber%      An open file.
    þ device$          "SCRN:", or a printer such as "LPT1:".

A new size clears the screen.  In SCREEN 0, 43 rows need an EGA or VGA and
50 rows a VGA; a graphics mode's columns are fixed.  A size the screen
cannot have is "Illegal function call".

Example:
    WIDTH 40                          ' big letters
    WIDTH 80, 50                      ' 50 rows on a VGA
    WIDTH LPRINT 132

See Also  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v  \aScreen Modes\vscreenmodes\v  \aPRINT\vPRINT\v  \aVIEW PRINT\vVIEW-PRINT\v
.context WINDOW
.topic WINDOW Statement
WINDOW gives the graphics viewport coordinates in units of the program's
choosing.

\bWINDOW\p [[SCREEN] (x1!, y1!)-(x2!, y2!)]

    þ SCREEN           y grows downwards, as on the screen.  Without
                       SCREEN, y grows upwards, as on a graph.
    þ (x1!, y1!)       One corner of the area, in the new units.
    þ (x2!, y2!)       The opposite corner.

The area is fitted to the viewport.  PSET, LINE, CIRCLE, PAINT, POINT and
the other drawing statements then take coordinates in the new units, and
PMAP converts between them and pixels.  WINDOW alone goes back to pixels.
Two equal x or y values, or WINDOW on a text screen, are "Illegal function
call".

Example:
    SCREEN 12
    WINDOW (-10, -10)-(10, 10)        ' (0, 0) is in the middle
    LINE (-10, 0)-(10, 0)             ' the x axis
    FOR x = -10 TO 10 STEP .05: PSET (x, x * x / 10): NEXT x

See Also  \aVIEW\vVIEW\v  \aPMAP\vPMAP\v  \aPOINT\vPOINT\v  \aSCREEN\vSCREEN\v
.context WRITE
.topic WRITE Statement
WRITE writes values on the screen or into a file in a form INPUT # reads
back: commas between them and strings in quotes.

\bWRITE\p [#filenumber%,] [expressionlist]

    þ filenumber%      A file opened for OUTPUT or APPEND.  Without
                       one, WRITE writes on the screen.
    þ expressionlist   The numbers and strings to write, with commas
                       between them.

Numbers are written without the blanks PRINT puts round them.  The line
always ends after the last item; WRITE alone writes an empty line.

Example:
    OPEN "PEOPLE.TXT" FOR OUTPUT AS #1
    WRITE #1, "Smith, J", 42, 1.5     ' "Smith, J",42,1.5
    CLOSE #1

See Also  \aPRINT\vPRINT\v  \aINPUT\vINPUT\v  \aOPEN\vOPEN\v
